## 1. Introduction
Water is one of the resources that human beings rely on for survival, but \(97.5\%\) of the global water resources is salty water, which could not be directly used as drinking water, and only \(1\%\) of the global water resources could be truly extracted and utilized by human beings [1–3]. A new research report released by the United Nations Meteorological Department in March 2020 showed that global water consumption has increased six times in the past 100 years, and it is still growing at a rate of approximately \(1\%\) per year. By 2050, more than \(50\%\) of the world's population is estimated to possibly suffer from water shortage [4,5]. However, under the influence of the rapid development of human's daily life and modern industry, water pollution has become an inevitable problem in the world. The halogenated pollutants, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, and other organic compounds discharged in the production process of paper, chemical, machinery manufacturing, and food processing industries not only cause a waste of resources but also seriously affect the sustainable development of the earth's ecological environment and human health [6–10].
Common inorganic contaminants (IOCs) in water include a variety of heavy metal ions (HMIs) ammonia nitrogen, phosphorus, sulfide, and fluoride. HMIs are highly toxic and carcinogenic, which could cause health problems in the brain, liver, kidney, bone and immune system [11–14]. Besides, HMIs are easy to be enriched and not easy to be degraded, thereby causing great harm to organisms. The World Health Organization's Drinking Water Quality Standard stipulates that the concentration of heavy metals in drinking water must not exceed \(0.01\,\mathrm{mg}\) of arsenic, \(0.003\,\mathrm{mg}\) of cadmium, \(0.05\,\mathrm{mg}\) of chromium, \(0.01\,\mathrm{mg}\) of lead, and \(0.001\,\mathrm{mg}\) of mercury per liter [15]. In terms of other IOCs, fluoride has two sides to humans. Fluoride is one of the essential trace elements in human body, however, the excessive ingestion of it would cause dental caries and skeletal fluorosis. Because of the special geochemical characteristics in some regions, the fluoride content in regional water source exceeds warning limit seriously, which lead to endemic fluorosis without effective treatment [16–18]. Effectively removing and recovering IOCs from water has attracted much attention. Given the advantages of simple operation, low cost, and high efficiency, adsorption is one of the important methods used to treat IOCs pollution in water [19–21]. Other treatment techniques include coagulation [22], electrochemical treatment [23], membrane separation [24], and liquid phase extraction [25]. The common adsorbents are metal oxides, activated carbon, siliceous materials, and zeolite. However, due to their own structural defects, these traditional materials in the removal of IOCs in water have limited adsorption capacity, a lack of selective adsorption, poor recycling, and other problems that need to be solved [20]. Researchers are increasingly considering the use of novel adsorbents to treat IOCs pollution.
The refractory emerging organic contaminants (EOCs) in sewage and industrial wastewater mainly include dyes, pesticides, and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, which all have specific toxicity to human beings, animals, and plants. Water bodies containing these pollutants could cause headaches, fatigue, and other symptoms when exposed to humans, in addition to various diseases and even death. At present, the physical methods widely used to remove EOCs include adsorption, extraction, and membrane separation. The chemical methods include wet oxidation, electrochemical oxidation, persulfate advanced oxidation process (AOP), and electrical float [26–29]. In recent years, with the fast development of modernity, all kinds of EOCs, such as pharmaceutical and personal care products as well as food additives, cause harm to people's health through wastewater produced from hospital, agricultural activity, or sewage treatment plant with
not-updated equipment and other channels. Considering the variety and complex structure of EOCs, finding an economic and efficient method that could remove EOCs from environmental water is one of the challenges at present.
In recent years, numerous studies have considered new processes to eliminate EOCs from water, such as ozonization, chlorination, extraction, sonodegradation, biodegradation, inorganic heterogeneous catalysis, and activated carbon treatment [1,30–34]. Amongst these, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been recently investigated for the removal of contaminants in water. MOFs are a novel class of porous coordination polymers, comprising inorganic nodes (e.g., atoms, clusters, chains) and organic linkers (e.g., carbohydrates, phosphonates, and sulates) that assemble into multidimensional periodic lattices. These materials have been proposed for various applications, including adsorption, catalysis, separation, sensing, fuel cells, drug delivery, light harvesting, photocatalysis, biocatalysis, etc [35–40]. Many advantages of MOFs making them promising material for water contaminants removal: (1) high specific surface area (e.g., the specific surface area of MU110 is up to \(7000\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\)), orderly spatial structure, and easily controllable pores; (2) enough binding sites (such as some unsaturated metal sites); and (3) formation of different functional groups by introducing organic ligands to optimize and improve their performance to remove various environmental pollutants [41–45].
In the last decade, MOF-type materials have been studied for environmental applications not only as selective adsorbent of contaminants but also as platform for their degradation through catalytic processes. From the first reported MOF in the adsorptive removal of a dye (methyl orange (MO)) from water in 2010 [46], or the first MOF in the catalytic degradation of an industrial product (phenol-pH) in water in 2007 [47], considerable work has been published. As a water purification material, the stability of MOF is a prerequisite for practical application. Although the structural collapse caused by the reversibility of coordination bonds in MOFs is a hidden danger (e. g., secondary pollution resulted by the release of toxic groups in MOFs or the desorption of contaminants), MOFs with high stability have been developed rapidly in the past decade [48]. Through the formation of high strength coordination bonds between positive metal sites and appropriate ligands, MOFs are able to keep their pores and crystal structures intact under different chemical and thermal conditions [49–51]. Much research efforts have been devoted to modify MOFs via controlling their pore diameter (adding functional groups and creating defects) or to construct MOF composites (associated with metals, graphite oxide (GO), perovskites, etc.), in order to engineer materials as stable and improved adsorbents and/or catalysts for contaminated water treatment. MOFs and MOF composites have been proposed for the removal of a wide range of contaminants, including HMIs, poly-fluoroalkyl substances, dyes, pharmaceuticals, plasticizers, among others [52].
While there have been reviews on the application of MOF in the environmental field, majority of them focus on heavy metals removal [53–57]. The summaries of both IOCs and EOCs removal by MOF are very limited [58,59], especially lacking in-depth elaboration on the removal influencing factors and the advantages and disadvantages of different MOFs with the most recent works. The number of studies in this field has dramatically increased in the past four or five years (more than \(80\%\) published within the last 4 years) (Fig. 1). Many excellent works have explored novel MOFs with specific structural modifications (such as the introduction of hydroxyl groups, amino groups, and mercaptan) or composites with other materials (such as magnets, GO, and carbon nanotubes) [60–62].
To fill this gap, this article reviews the underlying adsorption or catalytic degradation mechanisms of IOCs and EOCs in water by
MOFs, and discusses the best performing three-dimensional (3D)-MOFs and MOFs-based composites that have been tested to date for EOCs elimination. In order to give a broader picture of advantages and disadvantages of the use of each material, details of each framework and its properties are also included. Finally, the challenges encountered in the design and production of MOF-type materials as well as in their application in water remediation will be summarized, and the improvement directions are put forward for their industrial implement.
## 2. Mechanism of contaminants removal from water by MOFs
## 2.1. IOCs
Different interactions exist between IOCs (adsorbates) and MOFs (adsorbents). There are four common MOF adsorptive modes on IOCs (mainly heavy metal ions), namely, van der Waals force, electrostatic interaction, ion exchange, and coordination binding (Fig. 2). In practical applications MOF usually removes different adsorbates by a combination of various adsorption pathways. For instance, in the adsorption mechanism of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) with UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-PAM-PE composites, \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) is first transferred from the bulk solution to the solid–liquid interface and then adsorbed to the material surface through van der Waals force and electrostatic attraction [63]. This MOF contains a large number of amino, carboxyl, and amide groups, and it is able to capture \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) rapidly through surface electrostatic complexation and ion exchange. Similarly, the adsorption of ZIF-8@ZIF-8/PAN nanofibers on \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) also experienced electrostatic interaction, microporous filling effect, bonding interaction, and redox reaction [64]. The adsorption of SCNU-Z1-Cl crystal on oxygen anion (such as \(\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}\)) is not only related to the anion exchange of \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)but also includes the interaction between anion- \(\pi\) and wall structure and the adsorption properties of hydrogen bonding [65].
## 2.1.1. Van der Waals force
Given the characteristics of large specific surface area and high porosity of MOFs, van der Waals force generally exists in the adsorption process for HMIs by various MOFs [66,67]. As an
Given the characteristics of large specific surface area and high porosity of MOFs, van der Waals force generally exists in the adsorption process for HMIs by various MOFs [66,67]. As an adjunct to other adsorption pathways, van der Waals force usually does not play a significant role and is often not considered as a special factor [68]. In 2019, Liu et al. synthesized MOF-808 with a specific surface area of \(2152\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) on the basis of \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}\) [69]. The adsorption capacity of this novel material reached \(343.6\ \mathrm{mg/g}\) through the van der Waals force between \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) and MOF-808. Lv et al. prepared Ni-MOF that could adsorb As(V) via hydrothermal method [70]. Based on this, four new kinds of Ni-MOFs were synthesized at different pyrolysis temperatures \((300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}, 400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}, 500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) and \(600\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). The specific surface area of these five materials increased with the growth of pyrolysis temperature, indicating the magnification of van der Waals force, though less oxygen-containing functional groups on the material affected adsorption negatively. Due to the implementation of the equilibrium between van der Waals force and the number of oxygen containing functional groups, \(\mathrm{NiO/Ni@C400}\) pyrolyzed under \(400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) had the maximum adsorption capacity \((454.94\ \mathrm{mg/g}\ \mathrm{As(V)})\) among these five adsorbents.
## 2.1.2. Electrostatic interaction
When MOFs have charges in large quantities opposite to those of IOCs, electrostatic interaction is usually the predominant factor for adsorption. The direct factors affecting the electrostatic action are the surface charge properties and amount of MOF itself. Many researches demonstrate that the surface charge of MOFs is not only affected by the pH value of the solution but also related to the properties of the charged groups modified by MOFs [36]. Zhang et al. studied the adsorption mechanism of MIL-100(Fe) and FeBTC (iron-trimesic metal–organic frameworks using 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid (BTC) as coordination compound) on \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) and \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\), finding that the pH value of the initial condition was neutral due to the deprotonation of hydroxyl groups, and the negative charge of the two MOFs increased, thereby promoting the diffusion of metal ions on MOFs [71]. In addition, they found that the main peak of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) on XPS spectra (X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy) did not shift after adsorption, indicating that \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) did not form chemical bonds with MOFs and was absorbed through electrostatic interaction. Similar to the above experiments, Wang
et al. investigated the influence of \(\mathrm{pH}\) value on the adsorption of Pb (II) and Cd(II) by UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and found that the MOF was negatively charged when the pH value was greater than the isoelectric point (3.86) of the MOF [72]. They also revealed that the electrostatic interaction between the adsorbent and the adsorbent was enhanced, which was conducive to the removal of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) and Cd (II). The change in pH could lead to desorption of the adsorbate. In another work by Lv et al., MIL-101- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was protonated under acidic conditions and carried positive charge on the surface to remove Fe(III), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\), and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\), resulting in electrostatic repulsion between IOCs and adsorbents [73]. With the increase in pH value, the growth of negative charge on the surface was conducive to the electrostatic combination of the two. However, because the corresponding metal ions could be precipitated in an alkaline environment, the adsorption effect declined.
## 2.1.3. Ion exchange
The ion exchange between MOFs and IOCs occurs inside or on the surface of MOFs. The participants of ion exchange in MOFs are mainly central metal ions with the same valence as the corresponding heavy metals and various functional groups that could trap HMIs when they occur on the surface. A kind of \(\mathrm{Ca-MOF}\) could efficiently capture various metal ions \((\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}, \mathrm{Cd}^{2+}, \mathrm{Pb}^{2+}, \mathrm{Ni}^{2+}, \text{ and } \mathrm{Zn}^{2+})\) by ion exchange [74]. In this MOF, the electrostatic interaction between \(\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{II})\) and polycarboxylic acid ligands is weak, while the adsorbed ions could form strong covalent bonds with the ligands. According to the NMR test, in the pH range of 2–8, even with the presence of HMIs, no ligand was released in the solution, only the exchange of \(\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{II})\) and HMIs was observed. Liu et al. synthesized an eco-friendly \(\gamma\)-cyclodextrin metal–organic framework (MOF) based nanoporous carbon (\(\gamma\)-CD-MOF-NPC) material [75].
They found that \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\)initially bonded with the oxygen atom of carboxylic acid group. And the release of \(\mathsf{K}^{+}\)led to the adsorption site for Cd(II), thereby the ion exchange process occurred, which played a major role in the removal of Cd(II). Bahmani et al. studied the adsorption properties of \(\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{W I})\) and As(V) on chitosan-g-PNVCL/ZIF-8 nanofibers and pointed out that the adsorbents could exchange ions with the copolymers of amino and carboxyl groups through the micropores on the nanofibers, which promoted the removal efficiency of IOCs [76]. Wang et al. found that the hydroxyl that bounded to Al in MOF material MIL-96(Al) could carry out ion exchange with \(\mathsf{F}^{-}\) [77]. Accordingly, \(\mathsf{F}^{-}\)absorbed can be replaced by \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) from NaOH solution for regeneration. After the material was regenerated by \(0.2\,\mathrm{\mg/L}\) NaOH solution for 7 times, it still retained around \(70\%\) adsorption rate to \(10\ \mathrm{mg/L\,F^{-}}\) solution. Similarly, MOF-801 using Zr as the central atom also showed the ion exchange between \(\mathsf{F}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) in absorption process [78]. On account of this mechanism, the material had a better capacity of removing fluoride under acidic conditions, particularly when \(\mathrm{pH}~=~3\) , because \(\mathsf{H}^{+}\)constantly neutralized the released \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) constantly.
## 2.1.4. Coordination bonding
The adsorption capacity of MOFs can be enhanced by introducing highly active metal sites or reaction groups (such as hydroxyl, carboxyl, sulfhydryl, and amino) to coordinate with IOCs [79]. Among them, the amino group has a common complex reaction. It contains an unshared pair of electrons that form coordination bonds with IOCs, including Lewis bases [80]. In this case, the electron receptivity of IOCs could be controlled by adjusting the pH value [81]. Wang et al. used amino-functionalized Zr-MOFs to adsorb Pb(II) and Cd(II) and found that its adsorption capacity for Pb(II) was much higher than Cd(II), which may be related to their different affinity for MOFs containing amino groups [72]. Pb(II) has a stronger attraction to the lone electron pair on the amino nitrogen atom than Cd(II), because Pb(II) has a greater absolute electronegativity, hence easier to form stable complexes. Chen et al. also noted that although Fe-BTC basically does not absorb Pb(II), but after the introduction of amino group modified by DETA, it showed a good adsorption capacity for Pb(II) but did not adsorb Cd(II) and \(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})\) [24]. The reason is the same as the experiment mentioned above, that is, different HMIs have different coordination abilities with amino groups. Cd(II) and \(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})\) are easier to coordinate with amino groups than Pb(II), and the adsorption effect is considerably enhanced by coordination combination. Shen et al. studied the adsorption mechanism of Zn-BDC-modified magnetic montmorillonite on Pb(II) and pointed out that when the pH value was less than 2, the hydroxyl and carboxyl groups on the surface of the adsorbent were difficult to separate, thus, the adsorption effect was poor [82]. When the pH value was increased, the hydroxyl and carboxyl groups were more easily dissociated, and these dissociated groups could easily form complexes with Pb(II).
## 2.2. EOCs
MOFs mainly remove EOCs from water with two strategies: (1) MOFs as adsorbents (Fig. 2) and/or (2) MOFs catalyze the oxidative degradation of EOCs (Fig. 3).
## 2.2.1. Adsorption
At present, the commonly used adsorption materials for EOCs in water include zeolite, activated carbon, functional resin, and activated alumina [83–85]. However, with the increasing diversity of pollutants in sewage and wastewater, the traditional adsorption materials for the treatment of EOCs have some shortcomings, such as low efficiency, low adsorption capacity, and poor effect. As shown in Fig. 2, the adsorption of EOCs by MOFs is mainly realized
by electrostatic, hydrophobic, acid–base, \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions, hydrogen bonding, van der Waals forces, and coordination binding or a combination of these interactions [86–92]. Seo et al. used the \(\pi\)-\(\pi\) and electrostatic interactions between UiO-66 and methyl chlorophenoxy propionic acid to remove the latter from water [93]. It showed a high adsorption capacity, and its efficiency was close to 30 times that of activated carbon. The interactions between MOFs and contaminants depend on various functional groups, so the types of surface functional groups of MOFs play an important role in their adsorption performance. In order to synthesize functional MOF, direct synthesis, crystallization, postsynthetic modification, the solvothermal treatment, and ultrasound-assisted synthesis are often used to make MOFs surface has polar functional groups (e. g., –COOH, \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) , \(-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}\) , and –OH) [94–98]. Hasan et al. functionalized UiO-66 with \(S O_{3}\mathrm{H}\) groups by solvothermal synthesis. Based on the co-effect of electrostatic interaction, \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking, and acid-base attraction, \(18\%\) \(S O_{3}\mathrm{H}\) -UiO-66 had a significant adsorption effect on diclofenac sodium (DCF), and it still performed effectively at low DCF concentration [98].
Functional groups can adsorb contaminant particles depending on their surface charge. When the adsorbent is charged, it will produce electrostatic interaction. According to the difference of charge, both attraction and repulsion are possible. Therefore, many studies have adopted various methods to adjust the surface charge. Yang and Chen's group both integrated zirconium-based MOF material UiO-66 by phosphate modification and solvothermal synthesis to adjust the ratio of different ligands to synthesize UiO-66-P and UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) [99,100]. The results showed that after modification, the selective adsorption of cationic organic dyes was enhanced, but the ability of adsorbing anionic dyes decreased. In addition to the functional groups and surface charge, the pore size of MOF is also directly related to its adsorption process. The pore sizes of MOF vary from several to dozens of angstroms, and different pores allow MOF to adsorb and retain organic molecules with different molecular weights and spatial arrangements [101,102].
## 2.2.2. Catalysis
The adsorption process only transfers the contaminants from the solution to the surface of the adsorbent, and the desorption may cause secondary pollution. Therefore, factories and sewage treatment plants often use various oxidizing agents and free radicals formed by catalysts and certain reactants (such as ozone, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)) to further degrade EOCs [103,104]. For example, Fe-based catalysts (\(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) composite) are used to improve the decomposition of ozone and the formation of hydroxyl radicals, thereby degrading EOCs such as pesticides, herbicides, and dyes [105]. MOF has also been used as a catalyst to promote the formation of free radicals in recent years and has shown amazing degradation efficiency for EOCs under the conditions of light source irradiation (Fig. 3).
Under light irradiation, the organic components of MOF can absorb light and be stimulated to produce electrons. The photogenerated electrons are transferred from the highest occupied molecular orbitals (HOMO) of MOF to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals (LUMO), and then to the surface of metal-oxo clusters. This process is defined as ligand-to-metal charge transfer mechanism [106,107]. HOMO and LUMO are usually calculated by Hartree-Fock equations, semi-empirical molecular orbital methods, and density functional theory (DFT), which contribute to the in-depth understanding and improvement of MOFs' photogenerating electron process. Meanwhile, in the catalytic degradation process, highly active molecules, such as hydrogen peroxide \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and persulfate, are added to provide \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) and \(\cdot\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}\)free radicals, which could promote the oxidation of EOCs into products with lower toxicity through alcohol–aldehyde condensation, oxidation,
cyclopropanation, and other reactions under the irradiation of white light or ultraviolet (UV) [108,109]. The unique structure of MOF allows contaminant molecules to diffuse into its interior, making closer contacts between the two, thus providing more opportunities for pollutants and active species to collide and react [106]. It is worth mentioning that the adsorption and catalytic processes are not independent with each other. If the contaminant molecules are randomly dispersed in the environment, even though MOF has excellent ability to activate active species, it cannot show the satisfactory effect. Therefore, the ability of MOF to tightly immobilize EOCs is one of the prerequisites for its outstanding catalytic efficiency.
Compared with other catalysts, MOFs have the following advantages: (1) The crystal structure of MOF can be characterized and evaluated, which is beneficial to the further study of the mechanism of its photocatalytic process. (2) The rational design and fine-tuning of different organic active functional groups at the molecular level have a wide range of catalytic effects on the different EOCs degradation with a high degree of flexibility. (3) Different pore structures make MOFs highly selective. (4) Relatively higher degradation rate compared with traditional materials [110–113].
## 3. Recent studies on MOFs for the removal of contaminants from water
## 3.1. MOFs for the adsorptive removal of IOCs from water
## 3.1.1. HMIs
3.1.1.1. Arsenic. Arsenic is a widely existing metal-like element with strong biological toxicity and migration in the environment. When its content exceeds the threshold value of the human body, it causes chronic poisoning. Therefore, it is classified as a first-class carcinogen by the International Agency for Research on Cancer [114]. Arsenic exists in the form of As(III) and As(V) in water environment. The former is generally more toxic than the latter, and inorganic arsenic is more toxic than organic arsenic [115,116]. The summary on the recent advance of As removal in an aqueous environment is shown in Table 1.
Yu et al. found that Zn-MOF-74 containing hydroxyl group could efficiently remove As(III) and As(V) [118]. The Zn-O-As was generated through the coordination of Zn-O in the material with the adsorbate, which played a leading role in the whole adsorption process, and the adsorption capacities were 211 and \(325~\mathrm{mg/g}\) for As(III) and As(V), respectively. After desorption with \(0.5\;\mathrm{mol/L}\;\mathrm{Na}_{2^{-}}\) \(\mathrm{SO_{4}}\) (eluent), the material still showed a good recycling ability for As(V). The adsorption capacity decreased by approximately \(7\%\) (from 48.06 to \(44.50~\mathrm{mg/g})\) after three cycles at the concentration
of \(50\;\mathrm{ppm}\) . Plychompoo et al. synthesized a similar Zn-MOF-74 at room temperature and revealed smaller specific surface area \((45.4\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) brought by some differences in operation and raw materials [119]. After being composed with reduced graphene oxide (rGO) and polyacrylamide polymer (PAM), the specific surface area of the nanocomposite Zn-MOF-74/rGO/PAM (Fig. 4a–c) was nearly doubled \((81.1\,\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) compared with Zn-MOF-74, and the maximum adsorption capacity of As(III) increased to \(282.4~\mathrm{mg/g}\) . The composite was adaptable to a wide range of pH values and exhibited stable adsorption properties in the pH range of 4–10. The reasons may be as follows: (1) GO has excellent electrical conductivity and a large number of activities that could bind metal ions, thus considerably facilitating electron transport. (2) PAM also provides a large number of amino groups as binding sites. A notable detail was that the optimal adsorption \((282.4~\mathrm{mg/g})\) occurred when the mass ratio of Zn-MOF-74, rGO, and PAM was 4:1:1, indicating that MOF still played a key role in the composite. In addition, the interference of other coexisting ions in the solution to adsorption was investigated in the both above mentioned works, and consistent results were obtained that when \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) are present in the solution, the adsorption capacity of MOF is almost unchanged, but it is reduced by the presence of \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) (Fig. 4d and e), possibly due to the similar anion chemical structure of \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) and As which caused a competitive relationship.
Liu et al. used MOF (UiO-66) derived from zirconium 1,4- dicarboxylic benzene as a template to improve the activity of nanometer zero-valent iron (NZVI) [117]. The removal rate of As (III) and As(V) by UiO-66 was very low at approximately \(10\%\) , but the adsorption rate of As(III) by dispersing NZVI in UiO-66 was close to \(100\%\) . The ratio between the two had no obvious effect on the adsorption capacity of the composite. In addition, UiO-66- NZVI showed good anti-interference ability, and the removal rate of As(III) was maintained above \(98\%\) at the initial concentration of \(20{-}100\;\mathrm{mg/L}\) in the temperature range of \(15{-}30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and pH range of 3.0–12.0. It could quickly and efficiently remove As(III) in the solution of various coexisting ions \((\mathsf{C a}^{2+}\) , \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) , \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) , and \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\) ) and organic acids (oxalic acid and citric acid), indicating its strong specificity in the adsorption process of As(III). Assaad et al. modified UiO-66 with acetic acid (AA) and trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) with different equivalence ratios and obtained five kinds of composites capable of adsorbing As(V) [120]. Including the unmodified UiO-66, these six materials all showed the optimum adsorption performance at \(\mathsf{p H}=2\) . The adsorption capacity of MOF modified by TFA was generally stronger than that modified by AA. The higher the equivalent ratio of the same modified material, the stronger its adsorption capacity was. The introduction of TFA can induce the linker defects in UiO-66 structure and form free Lewis acid sites, thus exhibiting amazing selective adsorption for
arsenate. After five cycles of \(30~\mathrm{mg}\) adsorbent, the removal rate of As(V) in \(5\,\textrm{m L}\) solution with a concentration of \(150~\mathrm{mg/L}\) was around \(90\%\).
Atallah et al. found that In-AUBM-1 (AUBM: American University of Beirut Materials), a three-dimensional MOF capable of adsorbing arsenic, was of rare stability for MOFs [121]. The properties of the material did not change significantly after soaking for several days in the strong acid and alkali environment, and the quality loss did not occur until being heated to approximately \(150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Its surface was negatively charged in the pH range of 3.0–9.0, indicating that it did not adsorb arsenates simply by electrostatic interaction. Further studies showed that this MOF acidified the arsenate solution during the adsorption process (pH decreased from 6.7 to 4.3), suggesting that chemical acid-base interactions between arsenate and acid sites in the material may exist during the adsorption process.
Electrostatic interaction and coordination binding play crucial roles in the adsorption of arsenic. Adjusting pH value would strongly affect these two factors, thereby further affecting the removal efficiency. Significantly, a certain kind of MOF materials have redox activity, which can reduce As(V) to relatively less toxic As(III) in the absorbing process, however, there is a lack of studies on this property of MOF.
3.1.1.2. Chromium. Chromium is widely found in industrial processes, such as dye industry, steel manufacturing, leather tanning, and paint production. As a result of large-scale application, the emission of chromium into the natural environment increases year by year [122–124]. The valence states of chromium in water environment are \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) and \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) , which exist in the form of \(\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}\) , \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}\) , \(\mathrm{HCrO_{4}^{-}}\) , and \(C\mathbf{r}^{3+}\) , among which \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) is more toxic. The summary on the recent advance of Cr removal in an aqueous environment is shown in Table 2.
The pH value of the solution has an obvious effect on the removal of Cr in water by MOFs. Yang et al. found that the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) in water by \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}100\) decreased with the increase in pH value, and the adsorption efficiency reached the highest at \(18~\mathrm{mg/g}\) when \(\mathsf{p H}=2\) [125]. Under acidic conditions,
The pH value of the solution has an obvious effect on the removal of Cr in water by MOFs. Yang et al. found that the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) in water by \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}100\) decreased with the increase in pH value, and the adsorption efficiency reached the highest at \(18~\mathrm{mg/g}\) when \(\mathsf{p H}=2\) [125]. Under acidic conditions, the \(\mathrm{HCrO_{4}^{-}}\) in the solution tended to interact with the ionized central cluster \((\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0^{+})\) of the material. When the pH value was higher than 6.8, the adsorption effect of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) was not good, which may be due to the existence of OH- forming a competitive relationship with \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) adsorption. Wu et al. used \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\) and 2- aminoterephthalic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{2}A T P T)}\) to synthesize UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) , a nanomaterial that could adsorb \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) [126]. The functional group \(\mathrm{-N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) could be converted to \(\mathrm{-NH}_{3}^{+}\) in acidic environment, and the anion of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) could be adsorbed by electrostatic interaction. Therefore, the pH value of the solution has a great influence on the adsorption capacity of the material, such as carbonate and phosphate, which could increase the pH value of the solution through hydrolysis and interfere with the adsorption process.
adsorption capacity was approximately \(15\;\mathrm{mg/g}\), around \(40\%\) of the optimal condition. As seen from the above studies, pH and ions composition are two of the important factors affecting the removal of heavy metals by some MOFs. However, remaining within the optimal pH range or basically changing the ions composition of real water is impossible. Therefore, comparative experiments to confirm whether specific MOFs could be applied in real water environments are urgent and critical.
The variable organic ligands or composing materials of MOFs enable the material to remove the same contaminants through various processes. Zhu et al. found that \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{N-CNTs/N-G-800}\) with three-dimensional structure could reduce \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) to \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) by using formic acid [127]. In this process, formic acid was first dehydrogenated to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). The Ni in MOF has a strong enrichment capacity for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), and it could accelerate the formation of hydrogen atom through electron transfer and then reduce \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) to \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) by hydrogen transfer reaction. Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{N-CNTs/N-G-800}\) with rich porosity could provide a large number of active sites for \(\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}\), such as reducing nitrogen and oxygen groups with adsorption and catalytic functions, thereby showing an overall efficient \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) removal ability. Jamshidianfard et al. modified \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) with polyacrylonitrile (PAN), chitosan (CS), and the obtained PAN/ chitosan/UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanofibers, which increased the adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) by around 10 times (from \(32.36\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) to \(372.6\;\mathrm{mg/g}\)) [130]. A notable detail that with the increase in the mass fraction of MOFs in the composite material, the adsorption capacity of the material first increased and then decreased and reached the best at \(10\;\mathrm{wt\%}\). This finding may be attributed to the accumulation of excessive MOFs on PAN/chitosan, which reduces the specific surface area of the adsorbent, and also impedes the diffusion of metal ions in the nanopore of the nanofibers, thereby reducing the adsorption active sites. On the basis of similar ZIF-8, Yang et al. added different modified PAN materials and prepared ZIF-8@ZIF-8/PAN nanofibers (Fig. 5a–d) by in-situ growth of ZIF8, which had a large specific surface area \((871.0\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) and a suitable pore diameter (around \(0.6{-}0.8\;\mathrm{nm}\)) [64]. The maximum adsorption capacity for \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) was \(39.68\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) (Fig. 5e). In addition to the adsorption function, the nanofibers showed similar reduction principles to the materials studied by Samuel et al [128]. They were rich in active sites \((\mathrm{Zn-OH})\) that could reduce \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) and thus reduce its toxicity. The recovery of this material was poor, perhaps because the redox reaction left some ineffective active sites. After three cycles, the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) was only maintained at approximately \(80\%\) (Fig. 5f).
Our group synthesized a photoactive 3D MOF (Cd–TIPA) with a \(26.1\%\) porosity by cadmium ion and an elongated triangular rigid N-containing ligand TIPA (tBu-(4-imidazolylphenyl)amine) as strong electron donor (Fig. 6a) [131]. The result of Mott–Schottky measurements indicate Cd–TIPA is a typical n-type semiconductor. The more negative potential of the LUMO level (-1.30 V vs. NHE) in Cd–TIPA than that of \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}\mathrm{C}_{2}{}^{3+}\) further proves the possibility of reduction (Fig. 6b). The observable quenching effect of \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}\) anions on the emission of the suspension of Cd–TIPA indicates the efficiency electron transfer between them. As expected, Cd– TIPA was treated as catalysts applied in heterogeneous photocatalytic \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}\) reduction, which exhibited very high catalytic efficiency \((93\%)\) in 12 mins (Fig. 6c). A plot of the reaction progress - \(\ln(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{t}}/\mathrm{C}_{0})\) against time (min) fits well with the pseudo-first-order rate equation, giving a rate constant \(k_{\mathrm{obsd}}\) of \(2.1\;\times\;10^{-1}\;\mathrm{min}^{-1}\). The Cd–TIPA solids were easily isolated from the reaction suspension by simple filtration. After three runs of reuse, Cd–TIPA exhibited a moderate loss of reactivity from \(93\%\) to \(85\%\) (Fig. 6d). As to achieve the rapid and efficient detection and removal of trace Cr (VI) in water through different pathway, our group prepared \(\mathrm{Co}_{6}\mathrm{Zn}_{5}\mathrm{W}_{19}\) under hydrothermal conditions at the same year and explored its photocatalytic reduction for \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\). Attributing to the
injection of photoactuated electron, \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) could be almost reduced after \(90\;\mathrm{min}\) with the irradiation of \(10\;\mathrm{W}\) white light and the participation of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\). The sacrificial reductant \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) provided a huge number of delocalization electrons and \(2\mathrm{H}^{+}\)to promote the reduction process of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) [132].
Studies on the adsorption of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) by MOFs mainly focus on the capture of \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}\) and \(\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}\), which possesses great potential, while there are few reports on the removal of \(\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}\). Moreover, similar to reducing highly charged arsenic, how to transform \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) into less toxic \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})\) while adsorbing by MOF should also be further investigated.
3.1.1.3 Hydargyrum. The chemical forms of hydargyrum in an aqueous environment are mainly methylmercury and \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\). The former is more toxic than the latter, while the latter is more likely to enter the human food chain through crops and water. Long-term consumption of foods contaminated with \(\mathrm{Hg}\) could lead to symptoms of chronic \(\mathrm{Hg}\) poisoning, which is harmful to human health [133,134]. The summary on the recent advance of \(\mathrm{Hg}\) removal in an aqueous environment is shown in Table 3.
Liang et al. modified MIL-101 with \(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and revealed that \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101\) significantly improved the removal efficiency of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\). In a solution with \(\mathrm{pH}\) value of 7, \(10~\mathrm{mg~MIL}-101\) only reduced the concentration of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) from \(10~\mathrm{ppm}\) to \(9.21~\mathrm{ppm}\) (adsorption efficiency of \(7.9\%\)) [141]. However, the efficiency of \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101\) with the same quality under the same environment was \(90.8\%\). In addition, the modified materials could be applied to a wide range of \(\mathrm{pH}\) values, and the adsorption rates of the modified materials were maintained at above \(99.3\%\) when \(\mathrm{pH}=3{-}8\). The \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101\) adsorption of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) was also very selective. When the solution with \(10~\mathrm{ppm}~\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) and solution with
\(100\,\mathrm{ppm}\,\mathrm{K}^{+}\) , \({\sf A l}^{3+}\) , \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) , \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) , \({\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}\) , and \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) were mixed in the same volume proportion, the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) was still more than \(99.8\%\) . Similarly, Fu et al. synthesized \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) , UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) , and UiO-66-DMTD (DMTD: 2,5-dimercapto-1,3,4-thiadiazole) from UiO-66 [140]. The adsorption capacity and selectivity of UiO-66-DMTD on \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) was much higher than that of the other UiO-66 of the same type, with a maximum adsorption capacity of \(670.5~\mathrm{mg/g}\) (only \(59.3~\mathrm{mg/g}\) of UiO-66) [139]. After 10 times of recycling, the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Hg}\) ions decreased from \(98.9\%\) to \(85.4\%\) , and the adsorption capacity decreased from \(296.7~\mathrm{mg/g}\) to \(256.2~\mathrm{mg/g}\) . The reason for the decrease in removal rate is the loss
of UiO-66-DMTD in the process of experiment, but the adsorbent itself still maintains good adsorption performance based on its residual amount. Shi et al. synthesized a porous hydrophobic composite PCN-224/MF, which had a low absorption capacity of
\(94.4\:\mathrm{mg/g}\) for \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) [136]. The material was modified with thioglycolic acid to obtain PCN-224-MAA/MF, of which the absorption capacity increased to \(412.5~\mathrm{mg/g}\) . A notable detail that as the proportion of chloroform in the mixed solution increased from \(50\%\) to
\(96\%\) , the adsorption efficiency of the modified material increased from \(41.9\%\) to \(61.3\%\) , which could be explained by the hydrophobicity of MOF.
As mentioned above, sulfur-functionalized and nitrogen-functionalized MOFs tend to exhibit great capacity for removing \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) . In addition, the introduction of hydroxyl would also enhance the \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) adsorption performance of MOFs, and the adsorption process generally adapts to the pseudo-second-order kinetics and Langmuir model.
3.1.1.4. Lead. Lead is released into the environment through various industrial activities, such as battery production, electroplating processes, and the manufacture of microelectronic products. Excessive lead exposure could lead to mental retardation, encephalopathy, anemia, and other physical impairments, especially for children. \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) has no lone pair electrons and it could accept electrons containing oxygen and nitrogen groups to form relatively stable coordination compounds through chemical bonding [142,143]. It usually precipitates in the form of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) when \(\mathrm{pH}\) value is greater than 7. Thus, in the experiments of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) adsorption by MOF and its composites, the \(\mathrm{pH}\) value is generally less than 6 (Table 4).
Liu et al. prepared a hierarchical aerogel block UiO-66- \(\cdot\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\) -CS, which showed stable and effective adsorption performance for \(\mathbf{Pb}\) (II) through covalent crosslinking [148]. They explored the adsorption mechanism of the coordination bond formation between N and \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) and found that O alates played a synergistic role in the adsorption. Similarly, Mohammadi et al. studied the removal properties of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-SiO}}_{2}@C\mathrm{u}\) -MOF on \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) in natural water samples and
found that it almost did not adsorb common cations (such as \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) , \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) , \(\mathbf{Ca}^{2+}\) , and \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) ) in natural water, but the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) reached \(83.7\%\) , which was related to the bonding between N and Pb (II) [144].
Jin et al. synthesized MOF-5-CMC coated with carboxymethyl cellulose, and the removal rate of \(45~\mathrm{mg}\) of adsorbent was \(96.82\%\) in a solution of \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) at a concentration of \(300~\mathrm{mg/L}\) [150]. After four cycles, the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) by MOF-5-CMC remained at \(79\%\) . By using magnetic materials and zinc-oriented MOF, Wang et al. found that modified Zn-BTC with magnetic carboxylated cellulose nanocrystals (MCNCs@Zn-BTC) improved the removal ability of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) by \(123\%\) compared with pure MCNC [145]. In a weak acidic environment \(\mathrm{(pH}\,=\,5{-}6)\) , the \(\mathrm{COO^{-}}\) groups on \(\mathsf{M C N C s@Z n-B T C}\) surface can bind positive \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) ions through electrostatic interaction. When the acidity was high (pH less than 3), the process was weak and the adsorption capacity decreased. Moreover, the composite showed good adsorb ability to other divalent metal ions, such as \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and \(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{II})\) , indicating its wide application range. Subsequently, Shen et al. prepared MMMT@Zn-BDC by enclosing magnetic montmorillonite with Zn-BDC [82]. They found that with the decrease in the mass ratio of MMMT to Zn-BDC, the adsorption capacity of the composite for \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) increased slowly. When the ratio of MMMT to Zn-BDC reached 2:1, the maximum adsorption capacity reached an amazing \(724.64~\mathrm{mg/g}\) . The influence trend of pH value on MMMT@Zn-BDC and its adsorption mechanism are very similar to that MCNCs@Zn-BTC, but the difference is that hydroxyl also participated in the coordination with the adsorbent [145]. After five cycles, the removal rate of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) remained at \(80.5\%\) .
Geisse et al. used thiophene-containing DUT-67 to remove Pb (II) from water in batch and flow conditions and found that the MOF could be cycled at least five times with negligible loss of adsorption activity [147]. The results showed that the MOFs containing S heterocyclic polyformaldehyde had better Pb(II) adsorption than those containing benzene polyformaldehyde, and the MOFs containing aromatic thiophene demonstrated better Pb(II) adsorption than the MOFs containing non-aromatic tetrahydrothiophene. The existence of \(\mathfrak{t}(5\%\mathrm{)}\) -thiophene-Pb(II) complex was also proven for the first time by magnetic nuclear resonance. By using different aromatic ligands, Wang et al. prepared three kinds of MOFs with exceptional Pb(II) adsorbing ability, namely ZIF-8, MOF-5, and IRMOF-3, of which the adsorption capacities on Pb (II) were 449, 211, and \(75~\mathrm{mg/g}\) , respectively [146]. According to nitrogen adsorption analysis, the pore volume of IRMOF-3 was smaller than that of MOF-5 and ZIF-8, and the difference in adsorption capacity may be caused by different pore volumes.
Lu et al. synthesized MIL-101(Fe) by hydrothermal method and modified it using GO to form MIL-101(Fe)/GO with a special sandwich structure [149]. After modification, the specific surface area of the MOF decreased to nearly one fifth, but the adsorption capacity on Pb(II) increased by \(80\%\) . When \(100~\mathrm{mg}\) of MIL-101(Fe)/GO was placed into \(100~\mathrm{~mL~}\) Pb(II) solution with a concentration of \(300~\mathrm{{mg/L}}\) , the reaction reached equilibrium within \(15\,\mathrm{\min}\) . Lv et al. also added amino groups on the basis of MIL-101(Fe) to obtain \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , whose adsorption capacity almost doubled [73]. The material could detect Fe(III), \({\mathsf{C u}}(\Pi)\) , and \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) via fluorescence emission in amino organic bonding, with detection limits of 1.8, 1.6, and \(5.2~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) , respectively.
\(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) could accept electrons from oxygen, nitrogen, and sulfur groups to form relatively stable coordination compounds. Most MOF materials for Pb(II) removal can be improved by adding these groups. However, this approach would slightly affect the specificity of MOF adsorption of \(\mathrm{{Pb}(I I)}\) , which needs more research.
3.1.1.5. Other HMIs. MOFs have also been investigated for removing other HMIs, such as \({\mathsf{C u}}(\Pi)\) , Cd(II), Ni(II), and U(VI), although not as many related studies as the metals discussed above (Table 5). Wei et al. reported that f-ZIF-8@GO could adsorb \({\mathsf{C u}}(\Pi)\) with high efficiency [151]. When the \(\mathsf{p H}\) value increased from 2.6 to 3.8, the adsorption capacity was greatly enhanced, and the subsequent enhancement was relatively slow. When the \(\mathsf{p H}\) value was 6.0, the adsorption capacity reached the maximum of \(1872.24~\mathrm{mg/g}\) . The whole process is multi-layer adsorption through complex reaction, which conforms to Freundlich isothermal model. Most recently, Peng et al. found that the ZIF-8 membrane modified by polyaniline nanofibers could remove \(68.55\%\) , \(95.40\%\) , and \(97.75\%\) of \({\mathsf{C u}}(\Pi)\) at thicknesses of 30, 51, and \(70~\upmu\mathrm{m}\) , respectively, at a water flow between 6000 and \(10{,}000\;\mathrm{L}/(\mathrm{m}^{2}\times\mathrm{h})\) [152].
Zhang et al. studied the removal effect of Fe-BTC on low concentration Cd(II) \(\left(C_{0}\;=\;250~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}\right)\) in fixed bed and found that the removal rate was more than \(99.6\%\) , and the final concentration of metal ions was less than \(1~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}\) [71]. The adsorption rate of this material was faster and the reaction equilibrium time was shorter than those of other reported nanomaterials for Cd(II). In addition, domestic wastewater is generally weak alkaline, which contributes to the deprotonation of unsaturated benzenetricarboxylic ligands on Fe-BTC, because Lewis bases can easily coordinate with dissociative metal ions with empty electron orbits (forming inner-sphere complexation). Mohaghegh et al. attached a similar type of MIL-MOF to the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanotubes and obtained MIL88B(Fe)-Ag/TiO2 [153]. The maximum adsorption capacity of the adsorbent for Cd(II) was \(138~\mathrm{mg/g}\) , which was 1.6 and 1.2 times that of pure \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanotubes and Ag-deficient composites, respectively. The improvement of adsorption capacity is due to the fact that MIL-88B(Fe)-Ag/TiO2 has carboxyl groups, hydroxides, and carboxylate to promote chemisorption except for its high porosity and large specific surface area. The material also notably demonstrated the function of photocatalytic degradation of methyl orange dye.
In order to improve the ability of MOF to remove trace toxic metals (ppm level) from water, Li et al. immobilized ZIF-8 particles on the surface of trimesoyl chloride-modified polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) with the help of polyacrylic acid (PAA), and a new hybrid filter membrane PAA/ZIF-8/PVDF was synthesized [154]. The hydroxyl in ZIF-8 and PAA in this material could specifically interact with nickel ion Ni(II). The adsorption capacity of Ni(II) in high salinity wastewater was \(219.09~\mathrm{mg/g}\), and this concentration was obviously lower than the national standard. Tripathi et al. prepared a series of zirconium-based MOF derivatives by postsynthetic modification (PSM) of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and investigated its application in U(VI) removal (Fig. 7a) [155]. It was indicated that the monodentate coordination occurred between the phosphoryl groups on the surface of UiO-66-PO-Ph and U(VI), and the highest removal efficiency for U(VI) was up to \(96\%\) (Fig. 7b). The adsorption capacity of UiO-66-PO-Ph after reaction equilibrium was not constant and it slowly decreased (Fig. 7c), which may be due to the pore blockage of MOF materials, resulting in a decline in the total surface area and then hindering the spread of U(VI) through nanochannels. This phenomenon also led to poor recycling of the material, and the adsorption rate decreased by nearly \(50\%\) after two cycles (Fig. 7d).MOFs materials tend to initially remove HMIs in physical processes (mainly electrostatic interaction and van der Waals force), following by chemical adsorption (mainly ion exchange and coordination) to make HMIs bind more stably. The latter usually depends on pore decoration of functional groups with different polarity (amino, hydroxyl, carboxyl, and sulfonic groups). An in
MOFs materials tend to initially remove HMIs in physical processes (mainly electrostatic interaction and van der Waals force), following by chemical adsorption (mainly ion exchange and coordination) to make HMIs bind more stably. The latter usually depends on pore decoration of functional groups with different polarity (amino, hydroxyl, carboxyl, and sulfonic groups). An in
depth study of pore surface functionalization has become the main choice for MOFs to improve HMIs removal capacity.
## 3.1.2. Other IOCs
MOFs have also been studied in treating other IOCs in water, such as fluoride, phosphorus, and sulfide. Among them, fluoride was most extensively investigated one. Fluoride would cause significant changes in the ratio of fluorine to phosphorus in the bones, leading to bone deformities [156]. Though fluoride is beneficial to teeth to some extent, excessive intake can damage teeth. The World Health Organization recommends a maximum concentration of fluoride in drinking water of \(1.5~\mathrm{mg/L}\) [157]. The summary on the recent advance of fluoride removal by MOFs from aqueous environment is shown in Table 6.
Rong et al. used \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 with Zr-based centers to absorb \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\)in aqueous solution, and the removal mechanism was related to the affinity between \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\)and \(-\mathrm{COOH}\), since the content of this functional group decreased by two times after adsorption [158]. They also modified this MOF by hydrolyzing \(-\mathrm{COCl}\) to synthesis \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66-VG, which demonstrated a better removal performance. The removal efficiency increased \(32.4\%\) with the concentration of \(10~\mathrm{mg/L}\) for fluoride. In another work by Rego et al., a kind of Ce-based UiO-66 MOF presenting similar frame structure to Zr-based UiO-66 was prepared and used to remove \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) [159]. Although the removal mechanism (anion exchange with hydroxyl group) and specific surface area (around \(1100~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\)) was almost identical, the adsorption capacity of Ce-UiO-66 was higher (\(66.1~\mathrm{mg/g}\) vs. \(40.5~\mathrm{mg/g}\)). This may be due to the higher electronegativity of Ce
than that of Zr, as the former tends to attract electronegative \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) to metal nodes. Yang et al. implanted Ce(III) into MIL-96 to obtain Ce-MIL-96 through incipient wetness impregnations in order to improve its comprehensive performance, including adsorption capacity, stability, and regeneration ability [160]. It maintained a constant adsorption capacity (about \(19\,\mathrm{mg/g}\)) for fluoride in a wide range of pH value (from 3 to 10), and its adsorption efficiency remained above \(90\%\) after 7 adsorption–desorption cycles. Furthermore, co-existed anions in aqueous solution, such as \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\), \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\), \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\), and \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\), could not influence the adsorption of Ce-MIL-96 to \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) considerably. However, another MOF material, CeBDC-48 could be influenced by competitive \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) adsorption in the process of removing \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) [161]. The reason was that steric hindrance occurred when \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\) was multilayer-absorbed on Ce-OH active sites, hindering fluoride removal. Jeyaseelan et al. synthesized Ce@BDC and Ce@ABDC (adding amino) using nearly the same ingredient to the above material [162]. Both of these MOFs could defluorinate effectively in room temperature, but the latter performed better. It was because the electrostatic interaction between protonated amine groups and fluoride ion improves the removal capacity and efficiency. This may explain why amino groups can be found in various fluorine-removal MOF materials [158,168,169]. These results also indicate MOFs contained Ce might has a promising application in fluorine removal.
Zhu et al. prepared MOF-801 from zirconium tetrachloride and fumaric acid and studied its dechlorination effect [78]. The material could work stably under the conditions of a pH range of 2 to 10, a range of temperature range of 20 to \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and a series of common co-existed anions in aqueous environment. Although its adsorption capacity was general \((40.0\,\mathrm{mg/g})\), the stability of MOF-801 was very excellent under various conditions, making the for the design and preparation of the material could be as reference for other MOF adsorbents. Utilizing similar reagents and method, Tan et al. also synthesized a kind of MOF-801 [170] with
lower adsorption capacity \((17.33\,\mathrm{mg/g})\) due to the material they prepared had a smaller specific surface area \((522\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) compared with that of MOF-801 in Zhu's work \((725\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) [78]. In another study, Ke et al. investigated the application of adsorbent MOF-801 for the treatment of fluoride from tea infusion [157]. The material presented great thermal stability, as it could adsorb \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) effectively in boiling tea without the inference by high temperature. Moreover, it showed obviously selective adsorption of fluoride at lower doses \((2.0\,\mathrm{g/L})\), without removing catechins and caffeine from tea infusion.
compounds. The competitive adsorption properties of MOFs, which has not been paid enough attention, should be further studied.
3.2. MOFs for the adsorptive and photocatalytic removal of various EOCs from water
## 3.2.1. Dyes
Textile industry is a major contributor to global water consumption and industrial sewage discharge. In recent years, more than \(50\%\) of the dye wastewater in the world has been discharged by textile industry [172]. According to a report in Eco Textile News, the amount of highly polluting wastewater discharged by textile mills in Ethiopia far exceeds the limit set by the State Environmental Protection Agency [173]. Printing and dyeing wastewater contains dyes and other chemical reagents that are difficult or unable to be naturally degraded, which directly affect the transparency, aesthetic value, and dissolved oxygen content of the water. Moreover, the color of the dye wastewater blocks the passage of light through the water, and the lack of sunlight in the biota slows down photosynthesis until its growth is interrupted [174]. Humans have produced and used an estimated 5 billion kilograms of dyes and pigments, of which \(10\%{-}15\%\) has entered natural water bodies through discharge or drying processes. Many dyes and their decomposition products are toxic and carcinogenic to animals and
plants [175–178]. Therefore, how to solve the water pollution caused by organic dyes has become a problem of close concern all over the world.
Dyes could be removed from water by adsorption separation and catalytic degradation. At present, activated carbon, clay, graphene oxide, and other porous materials have been experimentally used in the adsorption of dyes. Electrochemical method is also a green method to remove dyes as it does not produce harmful byproducts or silt, but the degree of decolorization of cationic dyes is low [6]. Given the variability of MOF structure and performance and that its adsorption and catalytic degradation of dye could be implemented, current reports showed that the common MOF adsorption and catalytic degradation of cationic dyes include brilliant green (BG), methylene blue (MB), rhodamine B (RhB), and Direct Blue 86; anionic dyes methyl blue, MO, Congo red (CR), acid orange 7 (AO-7), sulfonyl rhodamine B (SRB), acid magenta; and a very few neutral dyes, such as methyl red (MR) (Table 7) [179– 181].
3.2.1.1. Adsorption. The main force between dye and MOF material is electrostatic interaction. Thus, most MOF materials in the current research achieve the purpose of removing dye molecules through adsorption. For instance, the ZJU-24(Cu)-0.89 loaded with –COOH groups can effectively adsorb MB, an endocrine disrupting
chemical, through electrostatic interaction with a maximum adsorption capacity of \(902~\mathrm{mg/g}\) [97]. Given that the dye adsorption of MOF material is completed through its own pore structure, the shape and size of dye molecules are related to the adsorption rate. MB, BG, and RhB are allocations in a solution, but the adsorption capacity of UiO-66 for MB is higher than that of the two latter dyes because the size of MB molecule is smaller and its shape is closer to the linear shape [100]. Another example is the adsorption of macromolecular organic dye Direct Blue 86. The layered UiO-66 (Zr) with a pore diameter of approximately \(40\,\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\) could effectively adsorb it from the aqueous solution, but the microporous UiO-66 (Zr) could hardly have the adsorption effect due to the pore size limitation [84].
The surface charge properties of MOFs also affect the adsorption efficiency of dyes. The molecular size of MB is smaller than those of CR and MO, but its adsorption capacity for UiO-66 is much lower than those of CR and MO [100]. Its reason was investigated because the former three kinds of dyes in the solution are in the form of anion, while MB and UiO-66 are in the form of cation in the solution. The electrostatic repulsion between the two leads to poor adsorption effect. So as the adsorption of MB, methyl blue, and isatin by MIL-100(Fe). Under the same conditions, the adsorption rates of the former two were \(97.9\%\) and \(44.9\%\) , respectively, whereas isatin could not be adsorbed due to their different surface
charge properties. MB is a cationic dye, methyl blue is an anionic dye, and isatin is a neutral dye [7]. Thus, the charge property on the dye molecule has a considerable effect on its adsorption rate.
Zeng et al. utilized different proportions of GO, UiO-66-NH\(_2\), and polyacrylic acid (PAA) as raw materials, adopted the method of vacuum filter self-assembly, and embedded PAA@UiO-66-NH\(_2\) between GO as a new composite membrane to combine the advantages of traditional adsorption material GO and MOFs (Fig. 8a-d) [182]. They combined the functions of the three materials through hydrogen bonding, electrostatic attraction, and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction and realized the adsorption of dyes MB, RhB, and CR (Fig. 8f). Through the contact angle comparison and pure water flux test, it was found that adding appropriate amount of PAA@UiO could significantly improve the surface hydrophilicity of GO membrane, thus enhancing its anti-pollution performance. The abundant oxygen-containing functional groups (hydroxyl and carboxyl groups) on the surface of the composite also provided a stable adsorption pathway for dye particles. The removal efficiency of PAA@UiO-1/ GO for the three dyes was close to \(100\%\) in the initial concentration of 10–50 ppm solution, and the removal efficiencies for RhB and MB were \(99.76\%\) and \(99.75\%\) , respectively (Fig. 8e). The adsorption performance of PAA@UiO-1/GO remained good when the pH value was 1–9, and the removal rate of MB and CR was still more than \(90\%\) under the extreme condition of \(\mathrm{pH}=13\).
Some dyes are electrically neutral in aqueous solution, making the formation of electrostatic interaction with MOF materials difficult. At present, no report could be found on the catalytic degradation of neutral dyes by MOF, and experiments of removing neutral dyes by adsorption are rare. Hamedi et al. attached MIL-101(Fe) to magnetic material \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) to synthesize MIL-101(Fe)@PDopa@Fe3O4 and studied its adsorption capacity for neutral dye MR [183]. They found that under the established conditions, the adsorption rate could reach \(89.9\%\) , and it could be recycled from wastewater by using eluent and magnet four times, which reduced the cost of MIL-101(Fe)@PDopa@Fe3O4. Although the above-mentioned MOF also has good adsorption performance in textile wastewater, the experiment was carried out in an acidic environment. The actual textile wastewater \(\mathsf{p H}\) is often alkaline, and even the pH value can reach more than 13. The adsorption capacity of MIL-101(Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{PDopa}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\) in alkaline environment will be greatly reduced, so it is still necessary to improve the environmental tolerance of MOFs from the basic structure.
In addition to electrostatic interaction, hydrogen bond and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction between hydroxyl group and amino group exist between dye molecules and MOFs. Some \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{MOF}\) materials, such as \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\ @\mathrm{AMCA-MIL-53(Al)}\) , adsorb cationic dyes MB and MG by electrostatic interaction, hydrogen bond interaction, and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction [187]. The maximum adsorption capacities at 318 K for MB and MG were 325.62 and \(329.61~\mathrm{mg/g}\) , respectively. Our groups synthesized an anionic 3D MOF (Mn-BTB) with a \(47.1\%\) porosity by combining on manganous (II) ions and a paddle wheel ligand, HBTB (1,3,5-tri(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene acid) (Fig. 9a) [188]. Cationic dyes (MB and RhB) and an anionic dye (MO) were examined for selective dye adsorption on Mn-BTB. Mn-BTB with an anionic skeleton was beneficial for the selective adsorption of cationic dyes (Fig. 9b and c). The adsorbent MnBTB could be reused at least six times with a moderated loss for MB in the adsorption capacity (from \(89.36\%\) to \(87.22\%\) ).
The adsorption of dyes by MOFs is mainly realized by electrostatic interaction. In addition, there are many reports about the
application of hydrogen bonding and \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction in this process. The selective adsorption of target dye molecules by MOFs is inseparable from their controllable pore structure. Therefore, the typical trade-off effect between permeance and rejection in the screening process should also be considered.
3.2.1.2. Catalysis. As catalysts, various MOFs, such as \(\mathrm{Co/Ni/Zn-}\) MOFs, MIL-53 (M) \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{r}\) , Fe), and \(\mathrm{UD}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , could be used for the photodegradation of dye molecules in the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) or other free radical reagents [84,189]. For instance, MIL88B(Fe), MIL-100(Fe), MIL-101(Fe), and MIL-53(Fe) in Fe-MOFs could be used in aqueous solution to activate persulfate to form \(\mathrm{.SO_{4}}\) and .OH, which in turn could decompose AO-7. Li et al. showed through experiments that AO-7 was first adsorbed to the binding sites on the surface (the adsorption capacity of AO7 by MIL-101(Fe) was up to \(153.4~\mathrm{mg/g)}\) and then degraded by MILs, and the highest degradation rate of the four MILs was \(93.1\%\) after \(240\,\mathrm{\min}\) [184]. Although the effect of MIL-88B(Fe) degradation of AO-7 was not outstanding in Li et al.'s experiment, other authors studied the degradation of MB by MIL-88B(Fe) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) composite materials [190]. Under the condition of adding \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and lighting provided by light-emitting diode, the degradation rate of MB reached \(100\%\) after \(2.5\,\textrm{h r}\) . This finding fully showed that different free radicals, degradation targets, and ligands have a substantial influence on the degradation efficiency of MOFs.
Among the MOFs, MIL-53 is well known for its amazing thermal stability and moisture resistance, and MIL-53(Al) and MIL-53(Fe) are the representatives. Chatterjee et al. prepared \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) (Al-Fe)- supported binary MOF MIL-53(Al-Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) , with pulverized coal aluminum foil and ferric chloride as raw materials to integrate their advantages of non-toxicity and strong photosensitivity [185]. The authors also studied its photocatalytic degradation performance on MB under different light conditions. Under visiblelight irradiation, the MB with initial concentrations of 10 and \(30~\mathrm{{mg/L}}\) was degraded by MIL-53(Al-Fe) \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) by \(89.34\%\) and
\(54.87\%\) , respectively, while the monomeric MOF MIL-53(Al) \(\mathbf{\nabla}(\mathbf{SiO}_{2}\) did not show any catalytic activity. Under 8 W UV irradiation, the degradation rates of MB were \(99.95\%\) and \(94.06\%\) by MIL-53 \((\mathrm{Al-Fe})@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) , and \(72.42\%\) and \(67\%\) by \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al})@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) , respectively. Compared with the pre-composite materials, the coexistence of Al, Fe, and \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) inhibited the recombination of electrons and holes and reduced the band gap. Thus, the photocatalytic activity was considerably improved. \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) -TPD analysis showed that MIL-53(Al-Fe) \(\mathbf{\nabla}(\mathbf{SiO}_{2}\) had high acidity \((97.42\,\mathrm{\mmol\,\NH_{3}/g)}\) and evenly distributed high acid sites. The surface of highly acidic composite materials could absorb more \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) or water, thus generating more hydroxyl radicals for photocatalytic reaction. As the first step of MOF catalytic degradation is still adsorption, the adsorption capacity further affects the degradation efficiency. The adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al-Fe})@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) on MB was as high as \(681.782\,\mathrm{\mg/g}\) , around 1.6 times that of \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al})@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) , which was also one of the reasons for the higher degradation efficiency of MIL-53(Al-Fe) \(\mathbf{\nabla}(\mathbf{SiO}_{2}\) .
However, most MOFs that could photocatalyze dye degradation mainly absorb UV light, which considerably limits the use of solar energy. Some experiments combined MOF, semiconductor, and photosensitive initiator for environmental protection, using solar energy as the light source. Araya et al. combined MIL-53(Fe) with cationic resin succinic acid, and the photodegradation rate of SRB was significantly improved [186]. The importance of adsorption pattern for photocatalytic rate is obviously reflected in this work. The DMIL-53(Fe) modified by Amberlite IRA900 showed charge-based degradation preference, and MIL-53(Fe) demonstrated excellent immobilization effect on anionic dye SRB after modification. After \(120\,\mathrm{min}\) of visible-light irradiation with a wavelength of \(420\ \mathrm{nm}\) or higher, the SRB removal rate of pure MIL-53 was \(24\%\) , while the SRB degradation rate of modified DMIL-53(Fe) reached \(96\%\) .
The principle of dye degradation catalyzed by MOFs is very similar to AOP, which oxidizes contaminants by catalyzing the formation of free radicals from active species. In order to highlight the advantages of MOFs in various catalysts, how to improve the photosensitivity and light utilization rate of MOFs/MOF composites should be taken seriously.
## 3.2.2. Pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs)
As a new group of EOCs in recent years, PPCPs are often released into the environment through industry, hospital, aquaculture, agriculture, animal husbandry [191]. PPCPs could be widely detected in the global scope, and even low doses could affect the human health and the environment, including the destruction of the endocrine system and aquatic habitats [192–194]. Thus, effectively removing PPCPs from the environment is very important. Considering PPCPs are EOCs for the past few years, conventional processes at sewage treatment plants are not sufficient to remove them very effectively. At the moment, the known methods to remove PPCPs include conventional activated sludge process, biofilm process, and AOP [195,196]. The removal rate of some compounds is under \(40\%\) , and it could be as low as \(2.4\%\) in diuretics containing diatrizoic acid.
In accordance with their properties and uses, PPCPs can be divided into (1) antibiotics, (2) anti-inflammatory, (3) asthma drugs, (4) antibacteria, (5) analgesic, (6) antipsychotic, and (7) hormones. The removal of the most popular and concerned first three using MOFs was subsequently discussed (Table 8).
3.2.2.1. Antibiotics. Nowadays, water pollution caused by antibiotics has become a global problem. Medical researchers indicated that approximately \(50\%\) of antibiotics used worldwide are overused, and the rate is as high as \(80\%\) in China. The concentration of antibiotics in domestic waterwater ranges from \(100~\mathrm{ng/L}\) to
\(6~\mathrm{mg/L}\) , and the main sources include medical and aquaculture wastewater [193,206,207].
Xiong et al. prepared MWCNT/MIL-53(Fe) composite by loading MOF material MIL-53(Fe) with multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNT) [198]. Because the very large pore size of \(12.09\ \mathrm{nm}\) , its adsorption capacity on tetracycline hydrochloride (TCN), oxytetracycline hydrochloride (OTC), and chlortetracycline hydrochloride (CTC) were 364.37, 325.59, and \(180.68~\mathrm{mg/g}\) , respectively, at the initial pH of 7 and \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . After the used adsorbent was washed with ethanol and dried at \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , MWCNT/MIL-53(Fe) could be reused for four times with insignificant decline in performance. The adsorption capacities of this material for TCN, OTC, and CTC are 1.25, 8.28, 3.34 times higher than that of single MWCNT, respectively. However, at high pH, competitive adsorption on unsaturated metal sites may occur between \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) and contaminants particles, which leads to the obvious decrease in MWCNT/MIL-53 (Fe)3 adsorption capacities. In addition, this work found that the electron cloud density of benzene rings is an important factor which affects tetracycline adsorption. Indicating that larger electron cloud density of TCS corresponds to the stronger dispersive \(\pi{-}\pi\) adsorbate-adsorbent interactions.
According to the work summary in this section, it can be found that the ligand-to-metal charge transfer mechanism mentioned in Section 2.2.2 also plays an important role in EOCs adsorption. The calculation of HOMO and LUMO orbital energies is helpful to understand the fundamental contaminants removal mechanism by MOFs. In the meantime, Wang et al. proved that adsorption is an indispensable step in the catalytic process [204]. Many experiments have showed that the preparation technology of Fe-based MOF has been pretty mature so far, and the synthesis method has been systematically studied. Therefore, the future research should focus on combining Fe-based MOF with industrial
adsorption materials, or cooperating with popular biomaterials (such as bacteria and microalgae) to form an efficient system, considering the interaction between adsorbents and adsorbates to prepare more excellent composites.
3.2.2.2. Anti-inflammatory. Anti-inflammatory drugs are used to treat inflammation that occurs in response to tissue damage. The two main categories of anti-inflammatory drugs are steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). At present, NSAIDs are widely used in clinical practice, including aspirin, naproxen, diclofenac, and ibuprofen. They have anti-inflammatory, anti-rheumatic, analgesic, antipyretic, and anticoagulant effects and are extensively used in clinical practice for the relief of osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, fever, and various pain symptoms [208,209]. The residues of such drugs in environmental water mainly come from sewage treatment and wastewater discharge, which pose a threat to the health of livestock and human beings [210]. Therefore, in recent years, many
researchers have used MOFs as adsorbents to remove anti-inflammatory drugs in water (Table 8).
As early as 2012, Hasan et al. synthesized MIL-100-Fe and MIL-101 to remove naproxen by electrostatic interaction [199]. At the initial concentration of 10 ppm, the adsorption capacities of MIL-100-Fe and MIL-101 on naproxen were 88.0 and 92.1 mg/g, respectively. Sarker et al. prepared MIL-101_GnO (3%) (GnO = graphene oxide) by combining different proportions of MIL-101(Cr) with GnO to adsorb naproxen in water [200]. The adsorption capacity of naproxen was 171 mg/g in acidic environment with pH = 5.4, which was 2.1 and 1.4 times that of commercial activated carbon and pure MIL-101, respectively. Naproxen exists in the form of deprotonation at pH greater than 4.2, which will generate electrostatic repulsion with deprotonated carboxylic groups in GnO/MOF composite, so electrostatic interaction was not the only adsorption mechanism here. On the basis of reference to previous works and contrast experiments (adjust pH value), it was found that the enhancement was also attributed to the formation of hydrogen bonds between MIL-101_GnO (3%) and naproxen. MIL-101_GnO
\((3\%)\) could be reused by washing with ethanol, and its continuous use did not result in significant performance decline.
Pena-Velasco et al. prepared MIL-101(Fe) by microwave-assisted method and fixed it on polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF), which could simultaneously remove naproxen, ibuprofen, and diclofenac through solid phase extraction (SPE) [201]. A pore size of \(29\,\mathrm{\AA}\) and wide particle size distribution \((0.48{-}10\ \mathrm{nm})\) significantly improved its adsorption effect on NSAIDs (Fig. 10a–c). The incorporation of PVDF hybrid membrane improved the permeability of the composites, and the broader size distribution was also one of the possible reasons for the enhanced adsorption of MIL-101(Fe) to NSAIDs (the diameters of NSAIDs molecules ranged from 0.6 to \(1.2\,\mathrm{\nm}\) ). Similar to the conclusions of Sarker et al., the adsorption rates of diclofenac and ibuprofen were significantly enhanced by MIL-100(Fe)/PVDF membrane, reaching \(100\%\) compared with those by pure MIL-100(Fe) or PVDF membrane, and the extraction rate of naproxen was \(85\%\) (Fig. 10d) [200,201].
Through the above research results, we can know two pathways for MOFs to efficiently adsorb contaminants: (1) Joint adsorption by MOF carriers and other composite materials. (2) Suitable pore distribution and high permeability of MOFs.
3.2.2.3. Asthma drug. Asthma is a chronic disease that the elderly and young children with low immune function in modern society are prone to. Asthma drugs can relieve wheezing symptoms during the onset, but they pose a threat to human health because of their
toxicity and presence in environmental water [211]. When the serum concentration of asthma drugs such as aminophylline and theophylline is more than \(20~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) , it could cause tachycardia and arrhythmia [212].
3.2.2.3. Asthma drug. Asthma is a chronic disease that the elderly and young children with low immune function in modern society are prone to. Asthma drugs can relieve wheezing symptoms during the onset, but they pose a threat to human health because of their toxicity and presence in environmental water [211]. When the serum concentration of asthma drugs such as aminophylline and theophylline is more than \(20~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\), it could cause tachycardia and arrhythmia [212].Liang et al. stirred \(100\;\mathrm{mg}\) of typical MOF material MIL-100(Fe) with a certain amount of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{POCl}_{4}\) solution, \(0.0333\ \mathrm{~g~}\) of polyvinylpyrrolidone, \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol, and \(12~\mathrm{mL}\) water for \(15\,\mathrm{\min}\) and then refluxed at \(90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{~hr~}\) to prepare \(\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})\) [205]. The introduction of Pd nanoparticles can minimize the recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs through their catalysis effect, thereby improving the ability of MOF to mineralize EOCs. The material showed a high photocatalytic activity \((99.5\%)\) for theophylline degradation. Meanwhile, Pd is scarce and expensive as one of the precious metals, so it is necessary to find a substance with properties similar to Pd but cheaper to replace it. Lee et al. compared the adsorption conditions of theophylline with different MOFs of UiO-66, \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) [202]. When the concentration of the adsorbent was \(0.5\,\mathrm{\mg/mL}\), the adsorption capacity of UiO-66 on theophylline was the highest at \(24.25~\mathrm{mg/g}\), while those of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) were 14.43 and \(11.12\ \mathrm{~mg/g}\), respectively. Although \(\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) demonstrated the largest specific surface area \((1484\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\), its window size \((5\,\mathrm{\AA})\) was smaller than that of UiO-66 \((7\,\mathrm{\AA})\). Besides, the addition of ammonium hydroxide in \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) did not reduce the thermal stability of the material, but the specific surface area, pore volume, and pore size were decreased.
Yang et al. synthesized classical zirconium-based MOF (UiO-66) by hydrothermal method to detect and remove clenbuterol (CLB) [203]. The LOD of UiO-66 for CLB in water was found to be \(0.17\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\), and the adsorption efficiency of CLB was more than \(80\%\). However, after \(-\mathsf{S O}_{3}\mathsf{H}\) was introduced on MOF, the adsorption efficiency of UiO-66- \(\mathsf{-S O}_{3}\mathsf{H}\) on CLB was only less than \(40\%\). According to the experimental results of Lee and Yang, not all ligands have a positive effect on the performance and stability of MOFs, more researches are needed to determine which ligand could improve the performance of MOFs on specific contaminants [202,203].
## 3.2.3. Pesticides
With the rapid development of rural economy and rural industry, the waste coming from agricultural production and livestock agriculture waste emissions increase gradually. The wrong or excessive use of pesticides by many farmers to increase crop yields lead to the low utilization rate of pesticides. In addition, pesticide residues act as persistent organic pollutants, enter surface water and groundwater, such as rivers, ponds, reservoirs, and lakes with surface runoff, during irrigation and rainy season [213–215]. Up to \(50\%{-}70\%\) of water resources are contaminated by agricultural activities [216]. In addition to causing serious water pollution, pesticides can enhance the resistance of bacteria and pests, kill beneficial creatures, and do harm to the health of animals and human beings, such as cancer, cardiovascular disease, and even death [217–219]. Many researchers have used MOFs to remove pesticides in water (Table 9).
3.2.3.1. Paraquat. Paraquat is a fast-acting bipyridine herbicide often used to control various annual weeds in orchards, tea gardens, and farmland. It could cause cell membrane, protein, and DNA damage and a great harm to human organs [225]. No specific antidote exists for paraquat, and its oral poisoning death rate is more than \(99\%\) [226].
Different from traditional MOF materials, Zhao et al. synthesized two-dimensional conductive MOF truxene-Cu cMOF with fantastic conductivity by using 2,3,7,8,12,13-hexahydroxy trichloromethane and copper ions as raw materials and modified its skeleton with a C3-symmetric molecule truxene [227]. They used truxene-Cu cMOF to detect paraquat in real water. Under the con
Different from traditional MOF materials, Zhao et al. synthesized two-dimensional conductive MOF truxene-Cu cMOF with fantastic conductivity by using 2,3,7,8,12,13-hexahydroxy trichloromethane and copper ions as raw materials and modified its skeleton with a C3-symmetric molecule truxene [227]. They used truxene-Cu cMOF to detect paraquat in real water. Under the condition of \(\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{N}=3\), the LOD was as low as \(4.1\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{M}\), proving the material is very sensitive to paraquat. This study innovatively adjusts the intrinsic electronic properties of MOF by varying conjugated linkers, which further reveals the impact of organic conjugated linkers and broadened the scope of application and synthesis of MOF. Khodkar et al. also synthesized a new magnetic \(\alpha\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}\) for photocatalytic degradation of paraquat and changed the dosage of catalyst, pH value, initial paraquat concentration, and contact time to study the effects of these four parameters on the degradation rate of paraquat [223]. Under the conditions of \(\mathsf{p H}=7\), catalyst dosage of \(0.2\,\mathrm{g/L}\), and initial paraquat concentration of \(20~\mathrm{mg/L}\), the degradation rate of \(\alpha\mathrm{-Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL-}\) 101(Cr)- \(\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) reached the highest after \(45~\mathrm{min}\) at \(87.46\%\). However, when the dosage of the catalyst was increased to \(0.4\,\mathrm{g/L}\) and other conditions remained unchanged, the degradation rate of paraquat was reduced to \(64.58\%\). In the case of high catalyst concentration, the solution turbidity increased and light scattering could not cross all the available surfaces of the catalyst, thus resulting in the decrease in degradation rate. Many existing studies have explored the basic influencing factors for MOFs' efficiency such as pH, temperature, and initial concentrations of adsorbents and adsorbates, but few have paid attention to the effects of the physical and chemical properties of wastewater or experimental water, on which more future research should focus.
Sakee et al. prepared a series of MOF catalysts \(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) by hydrothermal method, sol–gel method, and impregnation method [224]. These catalysts were reacted under appropriate conditions in a hot plate agitator at a speed of \(800~{\mathrm{rpm}}\). The results showed that except for the influence of pH, reaction temperature, and other conventional environmental factors, the proportion of Zn in the catalyst, synthesis method, sterilization, and other variables had a great influence on the physical and chemical properties of the catalyst, including specific surface area, pore volume, and average pore size. Meanwhile, the removal efficiency under UV light and sunlight differed. After controlling the above variables and repeated experiments, a conclusion could be made that hydrothermal method is the best process for preparing \(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). The optimal Zn loading was \(2\%\), the removal rate of paraquat under UV irradiation could reach approximately \(80\%\), and the maximum specific surface area was \(220~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\).
In Section 3.2.3.1, an innovative approach to tuning the intrinsic electronic properties of MOFs is mentioned. Then two aspects affecting the catalytic efficiency of MOFs are proposed. Firstly, the catalytic efficiency and MOFs dose are not linearly positive correlation, excessive addition of MOFs may lead to the reduction of catalytic effect. Secondly, the same kind of MOFs synthesized by different methods have different physical and chemical properties, which should be paid attention to in the preparation process.
3.2.3.2. Chipton and alachlor. Chipton (2-methyl-4-chlorophenoxyacetic acid) is extensively used for weeding in agriculture and it could easily migrate to the water body, and cause drinking risk of water resources due to its high solubility and fluidity [228]. Alachlor is a selective pre-bud herbicide chemically known as 2-chloro-N-(2,6-diethylphenyl)-N-(methoxymethyl) acetamide, with minimal volatility and resistance to UV decomposition. It could mutate animal DNA thereby is carcinogenic [229].
Liang et al. used a new method to prepare MOF@MPCA by loading ZIF-8 and UiO-66-NH2, two common MOF materials, onto carbon nanotubes and studied the adsorption properties of ZIF-8 and UiO-66-NH2 on chipton and alachlor [220]. The equilibrium adsorption capacity \(\left(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\right)\) values of ZIF-8@MPCA and UiO-66-NH2@MPCA for chipton were 110.4 and \(227.3~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively, while those for alachlor were 73.53 and \(107.18~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively. The adsorption capacities of MPA, ZIF-8, and UiO-66-NH2 for alachlor were 66.4, 75.7, and \(98.4~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively, indicating the adsorption rate of modified MOFs increased by \(45.8\%{-}131\%\). In this experiment, not only the microstructure and formation mechanism of MOF@MPCA aerogels were characterized by SEM, TEM, EDS, and XRD, but also the hydrophilia, compression resilience, and thermostability of the material were investigated. Since MOFs often contain toxic components, their dispersion in the environment will cause harm to the environment and organisms. The incorporation of MPCA greatly reduces the risk of leakage and accumulation of MOFs nanoparticles in organisms. This work provides a valuable idea for the safe and clean utilization of MOFs. Yang et al. constructed a series of diazo-linked Cr-MIL-101 derivatives via post-synthetic modification to effectively remove alachlor and enhance the photostability and photosensitivity of MOFs [221]. In \(40~\mathrm{~mL}\) of alachlor solution with a concentration of \(30~\mathrm{ppm}\), the adsorption capacity of the pesticides by Cr-MIL-101-C(1–5) through \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction and hydrogen bonding was \(100{-}150~\mathrm{mg/g}\), which was 2.2–3.8 times higher than that before the modification, and the removal efficiency reached up to \(96.9\%\). Compared with traditional MOFs, the diazonium moieties and phenyl radical on furan/thiophen-derived Cr-MIL-101 s were much more stable due to the effect of segregation. The novel synthesis method for MOF proposed in this work can be used to modify MOF by utilizing the C–C bond-forming without employing transition metal catalysis.
In the above reports, the 3D structure of MOFs was modified by material loading and covalent bond formation respectively, and the modified materials with high performance and stability were obtained, which guided the future work from different perspectives.
3.2.3.3. Pyrazole/pyrrole pesticides. Flusilazole, fipronil, chlorfenapyr, and fenpyroximate are pyrazole/pyrrole derivatives mainly used as herbicides and pesticides, and they have the advantages of high efficiency and low toxicity. These four pesticides have been recommended as a substitute for virulent organophosphorus pesticide, but they still show toxicity or other adverse reactions to nontarget biology. Improper use of them could cause soil and water pollution and harm to human health [230].
In 2016, Ma et al. prepared magnetic MOF as a magnetic solid-phase extraction adsorbent and first established MSPE-HPLC-DAD
method to detect and recover flusilazole, fipronil, chlorfenapyr, and fenpyroximate in water [222]. MIL-101 was synthesized in an autoclave with an appropriate amount of reagents, such as chromium nitrate, terephthalic acid, and hydrofluoric acid, and then the magnetic MOF material \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-MIL-101 was formed through the electrostatic interaction between \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) and MIL-101 under the action of ultrasound. The LODs of flusilazole, fipronil, chlorfenapyr, and fenpyroximate in the added standard water samples were determined by magnetic SPT to be 1.5, 1.4, 0.3, and \(0.3~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}\), respectively. In order to compare and analyze practical application of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-MIL-101, the four pesticides with different concentrations \((\upmu\mathrm{g/L})\) were added into three water samples of reservoir water, river water, and sea water to be used as water samples. The average recovery/removal rates of the four pesticides were \(81.8\%{-}107.5\%\) (relative standard deviation of \(1.1\%{-}7.8\%\)) in the reservoir water samples, \(81.0\%{-}59.5\%\) (relative standard deviation of \(1.8\%{-}7.7\%\)) in the river water samples, and \(80.2\%{-}106.5\%\) (relative standard deviation of \(3.1\%{-}7.8\%\)) in the seawater samples. The data showed that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\)-MIL-101 has a great potential in the detection and adsorption separation of trace pyrazole/pyrrole pesticide in water, with high extraction rate and low LOD. The overall process is convenient, fast, time-saving, and environmentally friendly. Excellent performance was even observed in the real water sample.
## 3.2.4. Phenolic compounds
Phenolic compounds are used in the manufacture of bottles, vials, antioxidants, and additives, and they appear in every aspect of people's lives. Wastewater containing phenol is now one of the major industrial wastewaters, which can be derived from oil refining, metallurgy, machinery manufacturing, medicine, painting, and many other industrial emissions [231]. Long-term exposure to phenolic compounds could cause human irregular breathing, muscle weakness, and symptoms of coma. It also has a substantial negative effect on fetus [58,232,233]. The recent research on recovery and treatment of phenol-containing wastewater by MOFs can be seen in Table 10.
3.2.4.1. Bisphenol A (BPA). BPA is one of the most widely used industrial compounds and has been used since the 1960s to produce plastic bottles, baby suction cups, and the lining of food and beverage cans [241]. Studies suggested that BPA has embryonic toxicity and teratogenicity [242].
Ma et al. successfully prepared three kinds of nitrogen-doped carbon nanotube frameworks (NCNTFs) with different nitrogen contents and graphitization degrees using a common MOF material of ZIF-67 as precursor and studied their catalytic degradation of BPA [234]. The specific surface areas of NCNTFs-700, NCNTFs-800, and NCNTFs-900 were 406, 410, and \(264~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), respectively. When Oxone was added to provide peroxymonosulfate (PMS), the removal rates of BPA by these frameworks were \(79.7\%\), \(97.3\%\), and \(53.4\%\), respectively. Free radical quenching experiment and electron paramagnetic resonance measurements confirmed that non-radical process dominated BPA degradation over NCNTFs-800, and common free radicals such as \(\cdot\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}\)and \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) only play an auxiliary role. Secondly, DFT calculation also verified that PMS adsorbed on \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\)-conjugated carbon network tended to induce non-free radical oxidation process. The highest degradation rate of BPA by NCNTFs-800 was mainly due to the highest nitrogen content on its surface, the good balance between graphite nitrogen sites and graphitization degree on NCNTFs-800 surface was conducive to electrons transfer from BPA to PMS to induce the formation of non-free radicals. With the increase in the amount of catalyst, the number of active sites available for degradation increased, and the degradation efficiency of BPA improved. However, when the concentration of Clnone was exceeded, the
self-quenching reaction of PMS reduced the degradation rate and efficiency. Yang et al. synthesized magnetic porous nanocrystalline \(\mathsf{C o F e}_{2}0_{4}\mathsf{N C}\) with large specific surface area \((60.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) and developed a mesoporous structure \((0.64\mathrm{~cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})\) by using Co and Fe bimetal–organic framework as templates via one-step method [235]. In the all experiments, the initial \(\mathsf{p H}\) was controlled at 6.5 and the temperature was approximately \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , which provided a reference for the practical application of the \(\mathsf{C o F e}_{2}0_{4}\mathsf{N C}/\mathsf{P M S}\) system in the future. Under the addition of PMS, the free radicals . \(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{-}\) and .OH were produced in the \(\mathsf{C o F e}_{2}0_{4}\mathsf{N C}/\mathsf{P M S}\) system to further degrade BPA. Contrast test showed that in single MOFs, such as Fe-MOFs and Co-MOFs, the degradation rates of BPA were \(6.1\%\) and \(72.7\%\) , while the removal efficiency of BPA was only \(2\%{-}4\%\) for pure Co or PMS within \(60\,\mathrm{\min}\) . However, the simultaneous addition of \(\mathsf{C o F e}_{2}0_{4}\mathsf{N C}\) and PMS made the degradation rate of BPA reach more than \(97\%\) . The difference between the efficiencies showed the importance of synergy in terms of contaminants removal. The used \(\mathsf{C o F e}_{2}0_{4}\mathsf{N C}\) could be reused after being calcined in the air at \(400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) , and the catalytic ability was almost unaffected. The results provide a theoretical basis for the development of porous transition metal oxide catalysts activated by PMS, and expand the further research of high-performance magnetic separable catalysts.
Most recently, Zhao et al. used ball milling to prepare the bifunctional composite \(\mathrm{Bi}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{17}\mathrm{Cl}_{12}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})\) for the first time in to explore the performance of MOF composites in photocatalytic degradation of EOCs [236]. Under white-light irradiation, the material not only could activate persulfate to degrade BPA in the pH range of 3.0–11.0 but also photocatalyze \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) sequestration. The experiment investigated the catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Bi}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{17}\mathrm{Cl}_{12}\) , MIL-100(Fe), and BM200 (prepared by combining 200 mg \(\mathrm{Bi}_{12}0_{17}.\) - \(\mathrm{Cl}_{12}\) with \(200\ \mathrm{mg\MIL-100(Fe)}\) on the degradation of BPA under white-light irradiation. Under the conditions of \(340{-}660~\mathrm{~nm}\) white-light irradiation and the addition of persulfate, the degradation rate of BPA by BM200 after \(60\;\mathrm{min}\) could reach \(100\%\) , and the efficiency was significantly higher than those of the first two common materials. DFT calculation and UHPLC-MS analysis confirmed
that BPA was subjected to nucleophilic and electrophilic attack in BM200/light/persulfate system. The benzene rings of BPA were destroyed by \(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{-}\) and .OH radicals to form intermediates. Then catalytic intermediates could be oxidized and decomposed into low molecular weight compounds, such as short chain aliphatic acids and mineralized products of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) . The authors also clarified the synergistic effect of BM200 and persulfate under white-light irradiation through ESR technology. The composite structure between Bi and Fe may be beneficial to the transfer of photogenerated electrons, while white-light irradiation could be beneficial to the generation of more OH radicals.
This section mainly emphasizes the importance of combined degradation, whether the simultaneous addition of MOFs and PMS or the cooperation between active metals may have a positive effect on removal efficiency. Secondly, the ratio between the composite materials is also important to the performance of the catalyst.
3.2.4.2. \(p\) -Nitrophenol (PNP). PNP is increasingly and prevalently used in petrochemical, dye, pharmaceutical, textile, and other industries. It has a stable structure and participates in the process of bioaccumulation, thus posing a serious threat to animal and human health [243]. Excessive intake of PNP could cause symptoms, such as nausea, headache, and even poisoning [244,245].
approximately \(96\%\). In view of the fact that surface water, groundwater and wastewater often contain coexisting inorganic ions apart from the target EOCs, contrast experiments had been carried out in this work. The results indicated that although various metal cations had no significant effect on the degradation rate, high concentrations of \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\)and \(\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\)tended to react with .OH to form .Cl and \(\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{-}\)which were with low activity, thus reducing the photocatalytic efficiency.
Ren et al. prepared a solid MOF composite material (Fig. 11b) by loading zeolitic imidazole frameworks ZIF-9 and ZIF-12 on cellulose aerogels for the first time on the basis of the mechanism that MOFs activate PMS to degrade PNP and other contaminants [239]. In the degradation process, the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) in the hybrid aerogel was used as the ion donor, the PMS was effectively activated by ZIF-9@GEL or ZIF-12@GEL (Fig. 11e), and sulfate and hydroxyl radicals were generated. PNP was oxidized into intermediate products by the
radicals and finally converted into \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). ZIF@GEL could powerfully degrade \(90\%\) of PNP within 1 hr (Fig. 11a), and the degradation efficiency had no obvious change at pH values of 4, 6, 7, and 9 (Fig. 11c and d). In addition, through the free radical trapping tests and electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy analysis, it was indicated that \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) in ZIF-9 and ZIF-12 was able to activate PMS to produce free radicals, which degraded PNP under the effect of \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}\)dominance and .OH assistance.
Tran et al. believed that attaching magnetic particles to MOF is a necessary step for its separation and recovery from water [240]. Magnetic porous carbon (MPC) was synthesized via direct pyrolysis of MIL-53(Fe) precursor at high temperature and nitrogen. After pyrolysis, the specific surface area of MPC at \(225.3\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) was approximately 70 times higher than that of MIL-53(Fe) at \(3.2\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), and the former showed better porosity than the latter. After adsorption equilibrium was reached within 4 hr the adsorption
capacities of MPC and MIL-53(Fe) for PNP were 33.73 and \(10.40~\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively, and their average removal rates for PNP were \(73.8\%\) and \(52.9\%\), respectively. MPCs are typically derived from Fe-based MOFs. PNP is composed of aromatic rings that can provide \(\pi\) electrons and negatively charged hydroxyl functional groups, which can form \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction, electrostatic interaction, and dipole–dipole H-bonding with MPC. And consider the simplicity of their preparation, MPCs present very outstanding performance are promising adsorbents for PNP in water.
Li et al.'s and Tran et al.'s experiments highlight that the in-depth study of MOFs electronic band structure is not only important for the activation of active species, but also useful for exploring the interaction between adsorbents and adsorbates [238,240]. Besides, the sample recycle of used MOFs should also be fully considered before preparation.
3.3. MOFs for the removal of EOCs from water through other protocols
## 3.3.1. Advanced oxidation
AOP is considered one of the most viable options for environmental treatment processes, including Fenton process and sulfate-based advanced oxidation process (SR-AOP) [168,246]. Since MOFs have been widely used in AOP and some examples of MOFs catalyzing reactive oxygen species (ROSs) generation under light activation have been mentioned in Section 3.2, this section focuses on AOP without light irradiation.
Radical quenching experiments and EPR analysis showed that the redox cycle provided by metal sites (e. g., Co(II/III), Fe(II/III), and Mn(II/III/IV)) in MOFs could activate PMS into \(\cdot\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}\) and .OH [247–249]. Free radicals can react with organic contaminants such as redox and hydroxylation to convert the latter into non-toxic products \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). Compared with traditional AOP catalyst, MOF with high controllability is a good substitute, and can effectively degrade emerging polyphenyl drugs (tetrabromobisphenol A, carbamazepine, and norfloxacin). Co-based catalysts are commonly used to construct PMS activation systems, such as ZIF-67, but high leaching of Co limits their practical application [250]. Wang et al. synthesized amorphous \(\mathrm{CoS}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) nanocages (derived from ZIF-67) by introducing amorphous \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) onto the surface of \(\mathrm{CoS}_{\mathrm{x}}\) particles to overcome the above-mentioned problem and further used them as SR-AOP catalysts for sulfamethoxazole (SMX) degradation (complete mineralization of SMX was finished within \(6~\mathrm{min}\)) [251]. The possible degradation pathway of SMX was also proposed based on the theoretical calculation of Fukui function
(Fig. 12). Amino group in the benzene ring of SMX molecule was oxidized by ROSs to form nitro group, or the vulnerable sites in SMX molecule were attacked and broken by ROSs. The intermediates formed were further decomposed into low molecular weight compounds such as amines and carboxylic acids, and finally mineralized into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}\), and \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\). Wang et al. utilized the good mesoporous structure of \(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\) to compensate for the instability of Co-based materials, and Azhar et al. developed a water-stable MOF using NiP as shell through electroless deposition technique, which exhibited exceptional stability at both high temperature and strong acid/base conditions [251,252]. The result of ICP-OES showed that the slight leaching rates of Ni and P are \(3.37~\mathrm{ppm}\) and \(0.11~\mathrm{ppm}\) after 4 repeated tests, which also indicated that the NiP shell was able to protect MIL-96 core well. Moreover, NiOOH formed by the oxidation of NiP was also an effective catalyst for the activation of PMS, able to catalyze the formation of singlet oxygen, \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-}\), and .OH radicals in cooperation with the core. Based on these principles, MIL-96@NiP could completely degrade the dye RhB in \(60~\mathrm{min}\) and requires a lower activation energy \((26.8~\mathrm{kJ/mol})\) than majority of the other similar reported experiments.
Fenton process refers to the technology that Fe(II) decomposes \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to generate .OH and peroxide radical \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}\), and then further oxidizes EOCs [253]. It has been extensively used in the treatment of dyeing, oil-containing, phenolic, and coking wastewater. Particularly, MOFs as heterogeneous catalyst porous coordination network for Fenton process have attracted extra attention in recent years. Kirchon et al. prepared a robust family of MOFs named PCN-250 and replaced some Fe sites with Mn and Co to improve the removal efficiency of MB [254]. XPS showed that the isomorphic substitution of Fe with Mn and Co ions resulted in the electro-rich of Fe and lower binding energy (BE) of Fe \(\cdot2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\). The lower BE for PCN-250 samples allowed easier and quicker transition between different oxidation states during the Fenton process. PCN-250(\(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}\)) were able to completely degrade MB in \(720~\mathrm{min}\). Following the traditional Fenton process, electro-Fenton and ultrasound-Fenton coupling technologies have developed rapidly in this century. Compared with noble metal-based catalysts, carbon-based materials are more stable and more abundant, and the electrochemical properties of carbon-based electrode materials can be adjusted by doping heteroatoms [255–257]. Zhang et al. calcined nitrogen-containing MOF directly and prepared porous carbon doped with N to boost the generation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in the process of electro-Fenton [258]. By recording XPS spectra, it was found that
large specific surface area, porous structure, appropriate N content, and rational proportion of spi-C are the important factors affecting the ability of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production. In order to evaluate the decontamination ability of catalysts, the oxcarbazepine degradation efficiency of NPC-1000 in natural water was tested, and the removal rate of total organic carbon was monitored simultaneously. The removal efficiencies of oxcarbazepine and total organic carbon reached \(75.7\%\) (in \(10\ \mathrm{min}\)) and \(71.9\%\) (in \(60\ \mathrm{min}\)), respectively.
From the perspectives of the basic mechanism of coordination chemistry, this subsection describes the MOFs' application in Fenton process and SR-AOP, and clearly explains the steps of EOCs degradation into ROSs and the activation principle of PMS and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), providing a few reference for future research.
## 3.3.2. Biocatalysis
Enzyme is a natural catalyst with remarkable catalytic activity and high selectivity, but it is fragile with poor stability, especially under harsh conditions [259]. MOFs are an excellent platform for immobilization of biological enzymes owing to their ability to maintain biocatalytic activity and protect biomolecules from chemical, thermal, and biodegradation [260]. Compared with free biomolecules, immobilized enzymes not only show stronger tolerance to extreme environments, but sometimes even performance higher activity under the synergistic action of metal ions than free biomolecules [261,262]. The methods for enzyme binding to MOFs carrier mainly include surface immobilization, covalent linkage, pore entrapment, and in situ synthesis [259].
The pore sizes of MOFs need to be adjusted to improve the mass transfer efficiency and good enzyme accessibility. Li et al. encapsulated horseradish peroxidase (HRP) in macro-microporous MOF by post-synthetic immobilization method, and obtained HRP@SOM-ZIF-8 with low leaching and anti-chelation properties [263]. SEM images and PXRD patterns showed that the pore and crystalline structures of SOM-ZIF-8 remained unchanged after the enzyme encapsulation, which proved the strong stability of the material. In removal tests, it was found that the removal efficiency of HRP@SOM-ZIF-8 for cationic and anionic dyes was higher than that of free HRP, because the carrier platform in the former system could pre-concentrate the dyes through adsorption. Remarkably, HRP@SOM-ZIF-8 had a higher removal efficiency for cationic dyes, than for anionic dyes. Speculated from the electrostatic interaction, the composite was negatively charged in solution and was able to aggregate more cationic dyes in the pre-concentration stage. For the sake of making the enzyme-MOFs conjugate have good recovery and non-toxicity, Peng et al. constructed a simple, cheap, and environmentally friendly immobilization strategy of laccase through molecular docking, and further studied the targeted removal of alizarin green (an anthraquinone dye) by immobilized laccase [264]. The results of HPLC-MS and \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectra indicated that the final degradation products were benzoic acid and phthalic acid, and it was deduced that the \(\mathrm{C}+\) oxidation was the main degradation mechanism for alizarin green. The prepared conjugate not only showed high laccase activity and acid resistance, but also could remove \(95.86\%\) of alizarin green through the synergistic effect of MIL-68(Al) adsorption and laccase catalysis.
This subsection introduces MOFs' another catalytic degradation form (biocatalysis) and the methods of combining MOFs with biological enzymes. As a reliable platform, MOFs can not only protect biological enzymes from external environment attack, as well as improve their activity, which is a promising developing direction.
## 3.4. MOFs for the removal of multiple co-existing contaminants
Each kind of laboratory material has to go through a lot of trial and simulation before it can be put into practical application. Environmental remediation materials including MOFs must not only
meet the basic requirements of low cost, high efficiency, and stability, but also have the ability to simultaneously remove multiple contaminants. MOFs' ability of combining various contaminant removal pathways makes it stand out amongst the many candidates. However, when several kinds of contaminants coexist, the complex mechanism is difficult to be explained. Only few of the relevant reports discussed the mechanism of multiple co-existing contaminants removal.
For the purpose of constructing an all-in-one adsorbent, Gu et al. used bacterial cellulose (BC) to combine MOFs into a flexible and porous composite membrane through physical entangling effect [265]. As a structural reinforcing binder with strong adhesion and high mechanical strength, BC was able to uniformly entrap UiO-66 or MIL-88B particles on the stacked structure surface and parallel layers of BC matrix through mechanical entanglement and interfacial interaction (mainly van der Waals forces and hydrogen bonds between the surface groups of MOFs and BC fibers). A multi-component BC membrane with a thickness of \(155\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) was prepared by using UiO-66, MIL-88B, \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{MgO}\). Its adsorption removal rates for BPA, sulfate, arsenate, \(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) were all above \(98.5\%\). Wei et al. developed a multifunctional oxidized poly (arylene sulfide sulfone)/UiO-66 (OPASS-U) nanofibrous membrane via electrospinning technology, which could simultaneously remove emulsified oil, EOCs (BPA, MB, and methyl blue), and HMIs (\(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\), and \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\)), and maintained structural stability in corrosive and harsh environment (in dichloromethane, diluted sulfuric acid solution, and NaOH solution) [266]. The introduction of UiO-66 increased the specific surface area, negative zeta point, hydrophobicity, and photodegradation efficiency of OPASS-U, which was conducive to contaminants removal and separation performance.
It can be seen that MOF has good application prospects in the field of multiple co-existing contaminants removal, thus simultaneously removing both EOCs and HMIs. However, there are still many issues to be solved: (1) Although the composites may have excellent properties, it is unwise to blindly combine different MOFs or other materials together, which is a waste for materials and also not conducive to the wise progress of the research. (2) The mechanism is still unclear, most studies only focus on the investigation on MOFs adsorption kinetics and thermodynamics, and no one has explored the complex interaction forces and electron transfer mechanism between MOFs and contaminants.
## 4. Challenges and prospects in using MOFs for water remediation
As a new type of porous material, MOF shows an amazing potential in selective adsorption and catalytic degradation of pollutants in water. In recent years, an increasing number of researchers tended to modify or compose MOFs by introducing new functional groups, surfactants, polymers, and nanomaterials. However, there are still many knowledge gaps in this field. The followings summarize the current research lacks in the field of MOFs' development and application in IOCs and EOCs removal from water and puts forward prospects for future exploration (Fig. 13).
## 4.1. Challenges
(1) Stability and reusability. Due to the reversibility of coordination bonds between metal ions and organic ligands, some MOFs has poor stability and weak adaptability, which is easy to be damaged in wet or extreme environment (such as strong acid or alkali solution) and the adsorption capacity decreases sharply or even completely loses. It has also been proved that some MOFs tend to decompose under
visible-light irrigation. For instance, under long-term illumination (7 days), ZIF-8 could be eventually oxidized to \( \mathrm{Zn}_{2}(-\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3} \) [267], with the ability of ZIF-8 to generate electrons and free radicals obviously decreased, and \( 1.4\% \) of \( \mathrm{Zn} \) as well as \( 75\% \) of dimethylimidazole were released. The inherent fragility and powdery structure hinder its practical application in water treatment. MOFs with low packing density usually presents loose powder form, which will cause problems such as packed bed blockage, abrasion, and quality loss of MOFs in the downstream applications. The consequence of poor stability is that the reusability of MOFs is generally unsatisfying (often about only 5 runs).
(2) Toxicity of MOFs' components. Despite its versatile in water treatment, many MOFs are composed of toxic metals (e.g., Cd, Pb, and Co) and/or toxic ligands (e.g., pyrazole and MB), and if these components are released into the environment during treating, secondary pollution is to be caused.
(3) Lack of in-depth exploration on the basic reaction mechanism. Existing studies have focused on the synthesis of new two-dimensional or three-dimensional MOFs, or the combination of MOFs and biodegradation, but ignored the most basic and most important mechanism research. In order to really improve the efficiency of MOFs in removing contaminants, it is necessary to have a clear and systematic investigation on the degradation pathway, reaction types, and intermediates.
(4) Real wastewater application. In terms of the working conditions of MOFs, the compound wastewater from rivers and WWTPs has various comprehensive actions between substances and complex flow conditions, and the variables simulated in the laboratory (such as pH, temperature, and pollutant concentration) are far from sufficient to represent the actual environment. There are also other active molecules in real water, such as pollutant particles and metal salts, which may cause competitive adsorption on the binding sites of MOFs. Almost all the experiments were not carried out in real water samples, which is one of the challenges that MOF faces when placed into practical application.
(5) Degradation under irradiation of natural visible light. In the research of photocatalytic degradation of EOCs, most studies used artificial ultraviolet light as the light source, only a few of them compared the degradation rate of organic compounds under visible-light irradiation and the effect was poor.
(6) Industrialized continuous treatment process. For the purposes of improving the treatment efficiency, the flow in the sewage treatment plant often has a fixed flow velocity, and the friction caused by continuous flow is a challenge on the stability and integrity of MOFs.
Based on the imperfections in theory and practice, future research should strive to achieve the followings to realize the goal of industrially utilizing MOFs in contaminants removal from water (Fig. 13).
## 4.2. Prospects
(1) Systematic exploring the application of MOFs in real (or at least simulated) wastewater. This is one of the fundamental and critical steps, and only very limited studies have carried out the relevant experiments. There are few solutions even the performance of MOFs in the simulated environment are not ideal. Therefore, experiments can be carried out to investigate the effect/mechanism of composition and properties real (or at least simulated) wastewater on MOFs' performance. The variables include real contaminants' concentration ranges, the competitive contaminants, pH, and temperatures found in real wastewater.
(2) Enhancing the mechanical stability and integrity of MOFs in view of industrial treatment. Before practical utilization, MOFs should meet several standards of mechanical stability. The material needs to have sufficient mechanical strength and elasticity to withstand external deformation forces, abrasion and pressure variations, to maintain the inherent properties such as crystallinity, porosity, pore diameter and so on. To overcome this challenge, MOFs can be processed into various shapes, such as thin films, particles, monoliths, liquids, and gels. For example, two kinds of MOF gels, ZIF@GEL and MOF@MPCA aerogels, introduced in Section 3. 2. 3. 2 and 3. 2. 4. 2 have good compression resilience and integrity while efficiently adsorbing various organic contaminants. The bioactive MOF-embedded hydrogels synthesized by hydrothermal or solvothermal methods also showed excellent stability in ion release tests [268]. From the prospective of coordination chemistry, the water stability of MOFs can be improved by selecting specific metal ions and ligands. For instance, highly charged metal ions can form strong coordination bonds with carboxylate ligands [50]. Future research can try to explore more materials with excellent mechanical stability and integrity like some MOF gels.
(3) Selecting components with low toxicity and cost. In order to avoid the pollution caused by the disintegration or abrasion of MOFs, it is necessary to understand the biological toxicity of its components. First of all, the relatively low toxic central metals (Zn, Fe, Zr, Ti, and Cu) and low harmful organic ligands should be selected to prevent threats to the environment and organisms [269–272]. Moreover, three should be more focus on the identify/dimension on the biological
toxicity of MOFs while testing the performance. Considering the large-scale use of MOFs, the process of rapid mass production of MOFs and inexpensive raw materials must be explored to reduce the production cost. For environmental friendliness, organic ligands and solvents with low price and toxicity should be selected as far as possible and harmless treatment should be preferentially carried out (Fig. 13). Few chemical companies, including BASF and MOF Technologies, could produce MOFs at the ton scale at present, and these MOFs have been commercialized. BASF has fully optimized the solvothermal preparation of Basolite A520 into an industrially feasible hydrothermal synthesis, and MOF Technologies has been allowed to synthesize MTA1 (Al-based MOF), MTA2 (ZIF-8), MTA3 (ZIF-67), and MTA4 (HKUST-1) on a large scale [240].
(4) Carry out experiments using fixed-bed or photoreactors under continuous operation conditions to simulate the real scale implementation. For the catalytic degradation of EOCs, on the one hand, the band gap must be narrowed under visible light and the solar harvest efficiency must be improved, on the other hand, the free radical-producing compounds (e.g., \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and sulfate species), which need to be added, should be identified and minimized to be used in bulk in wastewater treatment plants (WWTPs). Both DMIL-53(Fe) and \(\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}/P D I@M O F}\) mentioned in this review have good performance under continuous visible light irradiation, but much more exploration on other candidates should be proceeded in the future.
## 5. Conclusion
Overall, great advance has been achieved in MOFs' application in the field of water remediation, and MOFs are considered as a multifunctional platform, as they not only work in contaminants elimination but also as detectors. MOFs contain a promising possibility based on their readily combination with inorganic and organic hybrid materials, and their structures and lumen environment are highly controllable. High efficiency and selective removal can be achieved according to the different composition, size, physical and chemical properties of contaminants in wastewater. This is also incomparable with traditional cleaning agents. However, this field is not yet mature as many aspects still need to be explored and improved continuously, and the crucial parameters (such as safety, cost, reusability, and industrial conditions) still need to be explored and evaluated in depth to make the large-scale application of MOFs.
